{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21510","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21510","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21510","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyukgun","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Hyukgun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seok Hyung","familyName":"Lie","name":"Lie, Seok Hyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universal Sample Complexity Bounds in Quantum Learning Theory via Fisher Information Matrix"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T02:51:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:19:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-09T23:34:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:17:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T00:57:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:28:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:39:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-29T00:19:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/8k5v-ddtw","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that the sample complexity required in quantum learning theory within a general parametric framework is fundamentally governed by the inverse Fisher information matrix. More specifically, we derive upper and lower bounds on the number of samples required to estimate the parameters of a quantum system within a prescribed small additive error, with high success probability under maximum-likelihood estimation. Notably, both the upper and lower bounds are determined by the supremum of the maximum diagonal entry of the inverse Fisher information matrix. We then apply the general bounds to Pauli channel learning and Pauli expectation value learning, which serve as representative tasks in quantum channel and state learning, respectively, in the asymptotic small-error regime. Furthermore, we identify the structural origin of exponential sample complexity in Pauli channel learning without entanglement and in Pauli expectation value learning without quantum memory by comparing the quantum Fisher information matrix and the classical Fisher information matrix. We then extend the analysis to an error criterion based on the Euclidean distance between the true parameter values and their estimators, deriving the corresponding upper and lower bounds on the sample complexity, which are likewise characterized by the inverse Fisher information matrix. As an application, we consider Pauli channel learning with entangled probes. We highlight two fundamental contributions to quantum learning theory. First, we establish a systematic framework that determines the task-independent sample complexity under maximum-likelihood estimation. Second, we show that, in the small-error regime, the learning sample complexity is governed by the inverse Fisher information matrix, which is the central quantity in quantum metrology that determines the ultimate achievable mean squared error."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"41 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21510","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T07:01:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21693","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21693","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21693","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiafeng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jiafeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huakun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Huakun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianmin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jianmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyi","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Zhongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TiMi: Empower Time Series Transformers with Multimodal Mixture of Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:51:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:36:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T03:22:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:25:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal time series forecasting has garnered significant attention for its potential to provide more accurate predictions than traditional single-modality models by leveraging rich information inherent in other modalities. However, due to fundamental challenges in modality alignment, existing methods often struggle to effectively incorporate multimodal data into predictions, particularly textual information that has a causal influence on time series fluctuations, such as emergency reports and policy announcements. In this paper, we reflect on the role of textual information in numerical forecasting and propose Time series transformers with Multimodal Mixture-of-Experts, TiMi, to unleash the causal reasoning capabilities of LLMs. Concretely, TiMi utilizes LLMs to generate inferences on future developments, which serve as guidance for time series forecasting. To seamlessly integrate both exogenous factors and time series into predictions, we introduce a Multimodal Mixture-of-Experts (MMoE) module as a lightweight plug-in to empower Transformer-based time series models for multimodal forecasting, eliminating the need for explicit representation-level alignment. Experimentally, our proposed TiMi demonstrates consistent state-of-the-art performance on sixteen real-world multimodal forecasting benchmarks, outperforming advanced baselines while offering both strong adaptability and interpretability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21693","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:50Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:26:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21219","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21219","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21219","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Branislav","familyName":"Kveton","name":"Kveton, Branislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samyadeep","familyName":"Basu","name":"Basu, Samyadeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhojyoti","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Subhojyoti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Leyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franck","familyName":"Dernoncourt","name":"Dernoncourt, Franck","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungchul","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sungchul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyun","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Seunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Puneet","familyName":"Mathur","name":"Mathur, Puneet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihyung","familyName":"Kil","name":"Kil, Jihyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuxiang","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jiuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nedim","familyName":"Lipka","name":"Lipka, Nedim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan A.","familyName":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, Ryan A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler","familyName":"Derr","name":"Derr, Tyler","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning-Based Personalized Generation for Users with Sparse Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-31T01:54:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T21:05:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:10:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM) personalization holds great promise for tailoring responses by leveraging personal context and history. However, real-world users usually possess sparse interaction histories with limited personal context, such as cold-start users in social platforms and newly registered customers in online E-commerce platforms, compromising the LLM-based personalized generation. To address this challenge, we introduce GraSPer (Graph-based Sparse Personalized Reasoning), a novel framework for enhancing personalized text generation under sparse context. GraSPer first augments user context by predicting items that the user would likely interact with in the future. With reasoning alignment, it then generates texts for these interactions to enrich the augmented context. In the end, it generates personalized outputs conditioned on both the real and synthetic histories, ensuring alignment with user style and preferences. Extensive experiments on three benchmark personalized generation datasets show that GraSPer achieves significant performance gain, substantially improving personalization in sparse user context settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21219","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:51Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:59:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21806","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21806","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21806","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxue","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xinxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhou","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Yanzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunrong","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Chunrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Where Agent Frameworks Fall Short: Examining Functional Challenges and Usability Concerns"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:34:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:45:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T04:30:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:23:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T16:17:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:54:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-06T07:48:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:33:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) agents are increasingly built on agent frameworks that provide reusable abstractions for workflow orchestration, state management, tool integration, and execution control. However, the quality of this infrastructure layer remains insufficiently understood, particularly its functionality challenges and usability concerns, as existing studies have mainly examined traditional deep learning (DL) frameworks or model-level agent failures. Therefore, we conduct an empirical study of 5,669 bug reports and 809 feature requests from five mainstream agent frameworks: AutoGen, CrewAI, LangChain, LangGraph, and MetaGPT. We construct a four-dimensional taxonomy covering 22 root causes, seven symptoms, 11 motivations, and six requirements, and map them to the five-stage agent lifecycle. Across the four RQs, results show an execution centered quality pattern shaped by semantic interface boundaries. Reported bugs mainly manifest as Incorrect Functionality (76.00%) and involve more API, configuration, parsing, and serialization related causes than DL framework bugs, while their associations remain sparse and stage specific. Feature requests mainly target Feature Enhancement (49.07%) and reveal structured needs for Orchestration Expressiveness, Development Delivery, Model Adaptation, and Tool Ecosystem. These findings call for quality assurance beyond crash based and tensor level testing, with emphasis on API sequences, structured LLM outputs, serialization boundaries, execution traces, and execution centered maintenance, offering empirical guidance for reliable and usable agent framework infrastructure."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21806","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:17:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21947","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21947","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21947","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sohan","familyName":"Venkatesh","name":"Venkatesh, Sohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashish Mahendran","familyName":"Kurapath","name":"Kurapath, Ashish Mahendran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tejas","familyName":"Melkote","name":"Melkote, Tejas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Algorithmic Blindness in Large Language Models: A Calibration Study of Performance Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:32:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:54:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T04:36:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:23:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T14:33:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:15:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-05T09:38:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-07T00:50:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-25T22:03:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:39:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate remarkable breadth of knowledge, yet their ability to reason about computational processes remains poorly understood. Closing this gap matters for practitioners who rely on LLMs to guide algorithm selection and deployment. We address this limitation using causal discovery as a testbed and evaluate eight frontier LLMs against ground truth derived from algorithm executions. We find systematic, near-total failure across models. The predicted ranges are far wider than true confidence intervals yet still fail to contain the true algorithmic mean in most cases. Most models perform worse than random guessing. The best model's marginal improvement points to benchmark memorization rather than principled reasoning. We term this failure algorithmic blindness and argue it reflects a fundamental gap between declarative knowledge about algorithms and calibrated procedural prediction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code available at https://github.com/sohv/algorithmic-blindness"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21947","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:52:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Br.AI.K"],"givenName":"Jean-Philippe","familyName":"Garnier","name":"Garnier, Jean-Philippe","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A General Equilibrium Theory of Orchestrated AI Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T13:21:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-13T10:29:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-14T01:20:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We establish a general equilibrium theory for systems of large language model (LLM) agents operating under centralized orchestration. The framework is a production economy in the sense of Arrow-Debreu (1954), extended to infinite-dimensional commodity spaces following Bewley (1972). Each LLM agent is modeled as a firm whose production set Y a $\\subset$ H = L 2 ([0, T ], R R ) represents the feasible metric trajectories determined by its frozen model weights. The orchestrator is the consumer, choosing a routing policy over the agent DAG to maximize system welfare subject to a budget constraint evaluated at functional prices p $\\in$ H A . These prices-elements of the Hilbert dual of the commodity space-assign a shadow value to each metric of each agent at each instant. We prove, via Brouwer's theorem applied to a finitedimensional approximation V K $\\subset$ H, that every such economy admits at least one general equilibrium (p * , y * , $π$ * ). A functional Walras' law holds as a theorem: the value of functional excess demand is zero for all prices, as a consequence of the consumer's budget constraint-not by construction. We further establish Pareto optimality (First Welfare Theorem), decentralizability of Pareto optima (Second Welfare Theorem), and uniqueness with geometric convergence under a contraction condition (Banach). The orchestration dynamics constitute a Walrasian t{â}tonnement that converges globally under the contraction condition, unlike classical t{â}tonnement (Scarf, 1960). The framework admits a DSGE interpretation with SLO parameters as policy rates."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:46Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-14T04:01:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21247","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21247","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21247","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Rubel","name":"Rubel, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laxman","familyName":"Dhulipala","name":"Dhulipala, Laxman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars","familyName":"Gottesbüren","name":"Gottesbüren, Lars","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajesh","familyName":"Jayaram","name":"Jayaram, Rajesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Łącki","name":"Łącki, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PiPNN: Ultra-Scalable Graph-Based Nearest Neighbor Indexing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-17T02:18:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-24T00:23:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T00:49:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-10T19:02:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-14T00:05:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The fastest indexes for Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search today are also the slowest to build: graph-based methods like HNSW and Vamana achieve state-of-the-art query performance but have large construction times due to relying on random-access-heavy beam searches. We introduce PiPNN (Pick-in-Partitions Nearest Neighbors), an ultra-scalable graph construction algorithm that avoids this ``search bottleneck'' that existing graph-based methods suffer from.\n PiPNN's core innovation is HashPrune, a novel online pruning algorithm which dynamically maintains sparse collections of edges. HashPrune enables PiPNN to partition the dataset into overlapping sub-problems, efficiently perform bulk distance comparisons via dense matrix multiplication kernels, and stream a subset of the edges into HashPrune. HashPrune guarantees bounded memory during index construction which permits PiPNN to build higher quality indices without the use of extra intermediate memory.\n PiPNN builds state-of-the-art indexes up to 11.6x faster than Vamana (DiskANN) and up to 12.9x faster than HNSW. PiPNN is significantly more scalable than recent algorithms for fast graph construction. PiPNN builds indexes at least 19.1x faster than MIRAGE and 17.3x than FastKCNA while producing indexes that achieve higher query throughput. PiPNN enables us to build, for the first time, high-quality ANN indexes on billion-scale datasets in under 20 minutes using a single multicore machine."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear at KDD'26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21247","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:33Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-14T04:01:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21556","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21556","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21556","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nivasini","familyName":"Ananthakrishnan","name":"Ananthakrishnan, Nivasini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meena","familyName":"Jagadeesan","name":"Jagadeesan, Meena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Power and Limitations of Aggregation in Compound AI Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:23:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:24:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-08T07:53:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-09T00:28:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When designing compound AI systems, a common approach is to query multiple copies of the same model and aggregate the responses to produce a synthesized output. Given the homogeneity of these models, this raises the question of whether aggregation unlocks access to a greater set of outputs than querying a single model. In this work, we investigate the power and limitations of aggregation within a stylized principal-agent framework. This framework models how the system designer can partially steer each agent's output through its reward function specification, but still faces limitations due to prompt engineering ability and model capabilities. Our analysis uncovers three natural mechanisms -- feasibility expansion, support expansion, and binding set contraction -- through which aggregation expands the set of outputs that are elicitable by the system designer. We prove that any aggregation operation must implement one of these mechanisms in order to be elicitability-expanding, and that strengthened versions of these mechanisms provide necessary and sufficient conditions that fully characterize elicitability-expansion. Finally, we provide an empirical illustration of our findings for LLMs deployed in a toy reference-generation task. Altogether, our results take a step towards characterizing when compound AI systems can overcome limitations in model capabilities and in prompt engineering."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21556","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:09Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-09T02:04:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21534","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21534","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21534","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yidan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiqiao","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Kaiqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyi","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Chenyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renliang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Renliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqiao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yanqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Cong","name":"Cong, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARLArena: A Unified Framework for Stable Agentic Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:43:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:22:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T23:51:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:58:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-04T04:31:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:06:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic reinforcement learning (ARL) has rapidly gained attention as a promising paradigm for training agents to solve complex, multi-step interactive tasks. Despite encouraging early results, ARL remains highly unstable, often leading to training collapse. This instability limits scalability to larger environments and longer interaction horizons, and constrains systematic exploration of algorithmic design choices. In this paper, we first propose ARLArena, a stable training recipe and systematic analysis framework that examines training stability in a controlled and reproducible setting. ARLArena first constructs a clean and standardized testbed. Then, we decompose policy gradient into four core design dimensions and assess the performance and stability of each dimension. Through this fine-grained analysis, we distill a unified perspective on ARL and propose SAMPO, a stable agentic policy optimization method designed to mitigate the dominant sources of instability in ARL. Empirically, SAMPO achieves consistently stable training and strong performance across diverse agentic tasks. Overall, this study provides a unifying policy gradient perspective for ARL and offers practical guidance for building stable and reproducible LLM-based agent training pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear at ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21534","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:36Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T05:03:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathalie C.","familyName":"Pinheiro","name":"Pinheiro, Nathalie C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Donghu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannah P.","familyName":"Menke","name":"Menke, Hannah P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aniket C.","familyName":"Joshi","name":"Joshi, Aniket C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claire E.","familyName":"Heaney","name":"Heaney, Claire E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed H.","familyName":"ElSheikh","name":"ElSheikh, Ahmed H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher C.","familyName":"Pain","name":"Pain, Christopher C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Surrogate models for Rock-Fluid Interaction: A Grid-Size-Invariant Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:34:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-23T20:13:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T00:34:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s00366-026-02353-x","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modelling rock-fluid interaction requires solving a set of partial differential equations (PDEs) to predict the flow behaviour and the reactions of the fluid with the rock on the interfaces. Conventional high-fidelity numerical models require a high resolution to obtain reliable results, resulting in huge computational expense. This restricts the applicability of these models for multi-query problems, such as uncertainty quantification and optimisation, which require running numerous scenarios. As a cheaper alternative to high-fidelity models, this work develops eight surrogate models for predicting the fluid flow in porous media. Four of these are reduced-order models (ROM) based on one neural network for compression and another for prediction. The other four are single neural networks with the property of grid-size invariance; a term which we use to refer to image-to-image models that are capable of inferring on computational domains that are larger than those used during training. In addition to the novel grid-size-invariant framework for surrogate models, we compare the predictive performance of UNet and UNet++ architectures, and demonstrate that UNet++ outperforms UNet for surrogate models. Furthermore, we show that the grid-size-invariant approach is a reliable way to reduce memory consumption during training, resulting in good correlation between predicted and ground-truth values and outperforming the ROMs analysed. The application analysed is particularly challenging because fluid-induced rock dissolution results in a non-static solid field and, consequently, it cannot be used to help in adjustments of the future prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":2,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:02Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-26T02:19:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21321","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21321","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21321","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jindan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jindan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhaoxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tayfun","familyName":"Gokmen","name":"Gokmen, Tayfun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Symmetric Point Tracking: Tackling Non-ideal Reference in Analog In-memory Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:41:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:04:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-22T20:22:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:09:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Analog in-memory computing (AIMC) performs computation directly within resistive crossbar arrays, offering an energy-efficient platform to scale large vision and language models. However, non-ideal analog device properties make the training on AIMC devices challenging. In particular, its update asymmetry can induce a systematic drift of weight updates towards a device-specific symmetric point (SP), which typically does not align with the optimum of the training objective. To mitigate this bias, most existing works assume the SP is known and pre-calibrate it to zero before training by setting the reference point as the SP. Nevertheless, calibrating AIMC devices requires costly pulse updates, and residual calibration error can directly degrade training performance. In this work, we present the first theoretical characterization of the pulse complexity of SP calibration and the resulting estimation error. We further propose a dynamic SP estimation method that tracks the SP during model training, and establishes its convergence guarantees. In addition, we develop an enhanced variant based on chopping and filtering techniques from digital signal processing. Numerical experiments demonstrate both the efficiency and effectiveness of the proposed method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21321","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:23Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:30:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21456","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21456","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21456","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuan","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Chuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Litu","familyName":"Ou","name":"Ou, Litu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"MacAvaney","name":"MacAvaney, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeff","familyName":"Dalton","name":"Dalton, Jeff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting Text Ranking in Deep Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.3; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:18:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:13:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-19T07:08:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:33:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep research has emerged as an important task that aims to address hard queries that need extensive open-web exploration. To tackle it, most prior work equips large language model (LLM)-based agents with opaque web search APIs, enabling agents to iteratively issue search queries, retrieve external evidence, and reason over it. Despite search's essential role in deep research, black-box web search APIs leave the behaviour of established text ranking methods in deep research largely unclear. To fill this gap, we reproduce key findings and best practices for text ranking methods in deep research. We examine their effectiveness from three perspectives: (i) retrieval units (documents vs. passages), (ii) pipeline configurations (different retrievers, re-rankers, and re-ranking depths), and (iii) query characteristics (the mismatch between agent-issued queries and the training queries of text rankers). We perform experiments on BrowseComp-Plus, a deep research dataset with a fixed corpus, evaluating 2 open-source agents, 5 retrievers, and 3 re-rankers. We find that agent-issued queries typically follow web-search-style syntax (e.g., quoted exact matches), favouring lexical, learned sparse, and multi-vector retrievers; passage-level units are more efficient under limited context windows, and avoid the difficulties of document length normalisation in lexical retrieval; re-ranking is highly effective. We further propose a query-to-question (Q2Q) method that translates agent-issued queries into natural language questions, significantly reducing the query mismatch."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the 49th International ACM SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval (SIGIR 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21456","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:42:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21889","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21889","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21889","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Otto","familyName":"Nyberg","name":"Nyberg, Otto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fausto","familyName":"Carcassi","name":"Carcassi, Fausto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Tugnoli","name":"Tugnoli, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Cinà","name":"Cinà, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"2-Step Agent: A Framework for the Interaction of a Decision Maker with AI Decision Support"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:11:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:50:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T13:00:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:57:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T18:14:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-09T00:06:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predictions from ML models support human decision making in several fields, including high-stakes ones such as healthcare and the judiciary. Yet, we still lack a clear understanding of how decision makers learn from ML-based decision support (ML-DS). In this paper, we introduce a general computational framework, the 2-Step Agent, to capture this process. As a prediction from an ML model contains information about the training data, a prediction can also be used for inference. Our framework models (i) how a prediction for a new observation affects the beliefs of a rational Bayesian agent, and (ii) how this change in beliefs affects the estimation of causal effect, the downstream decision, and the subsequent outcome. In addition to the framework itself, we make three contributions. First, for the linear Gaussian setting, we derive a tractable solution for the challenging Bayesian inference problem we introduced, i.e. one in which the agent infers from an ML prediction. Second, we experimentally identify conditions under which ML-DS is beneficial. Third, we show that a single misaligned prior belief can be sufficient for ML-DS to lead to worse downstream outcomes compared to no decision support even when the ML model is well-specified and the agent is perfectly rational. Hence, even under ideal conditions, ML-DS can do more harm than good."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21889","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:40Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:19:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22041","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22041","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22041","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashwin","familyName":"George","name":"George, Ashwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vassil","familyName":"Guenov","name":"Guenov, Vassil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arkady","familyName":"Zgonnikov","name":"Zgonnikov, Arkady","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David A.","familyName":"Abbink","name":"Abbink, David A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luciano Cavalcante","familyName":"Siebert","name":"Siebert, Luciano Cavalcante","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using Feasible Action-Space Reduction by Groups to fill Causal Responsibility Gaps in Spatial Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:48:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:00:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-05T12:22:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:43:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Heralding the advent of autonomous vehicles and mobile robots that interact with humans, responsibility in spatial interaction is burgeoning as a research topic. Even though metrics of responsibility tailored to spatial interactions have been proposed, they are mostly focused on the responsibility of individual agents. Metrics of causal responsibility focusing on individuals fail in cases of causal overdeterminism - when many actors simultaneously cause an outcome. To fill the gaps in causal responsibility left by individual-focused metrics, we formulate a metric for the causal responsibility of groups. To identify assertive agents that are causally responsible for the trajectory of an affected agent, we further formalise the types of assertive influences and propose a tiering algorithm for systematically identifying assertive agents. Finally, we use scenario-based simulations to illustrate the benefits of considering groups and how the emergence of group effects vary with interaction dynamics and the proximity of agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Presented at COINE workshop collocated with AAMAS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22041","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:12:23Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:12:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-08T02:04:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sinan","familyName":"Bugu","name":"Bugu, Sinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Single-Shot Fidelity: Chernoff-Based Throughput Optimization in Superconducting Qubit Readout"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:21:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:08:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T18:36:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T02:02:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T14:55:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-05T02:03:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-10T17:15:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-11T01:13:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-02T15:01:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-03T01:05:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Single-shot fidelity is the standard benchmark for superconducting qubit readout, yet it does not directly minimize the wall-clock time needed to certify a quantum state. We treat the dispersive measurement record as a stochastic communication channel and compute the classical Chernoff information governing the multi-shot error exponent, using a trajectory model that incorporates T1 relaxation with full cavity memory. The integration time that maximizes single-shot fidelity and the time that minimizes total certification time do not coincide. For representative transmon parameters and hardware overheads, the throughput-optimal window is longer, cutting certification time by roughly 9-11%, with the gain saturating near 1.13x in the high-readout-power and high-overhead regime. Benchmarking the extracted classical information against the unit-efficiency Gaussian Chernoff limit defines an information-extraction efficiency: dispersive schemes capture ~45% at short integration times, dropping to eta_info(tau_rate) ~ 12% at tau_rate ~ 1.22 us as T1-induced trajectory smearing accumulates. These results connect readout calibration directly to the operational objective of minimizing certification time in high-throughput superconducting processors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:42Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-03T03:14:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21340","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21340","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21340","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack","familyName":"Goffinet","name":"Goffinet, Jack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Casey","familyName":"Hanks","name":"Hanks, Casey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David E.","familyName":"Carlson","name":"Carlson, David E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HiPPO Zoo: Explicit Memory Mechanisms for Interpretable State Space Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:16:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:06:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-29T17:13:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T01:16:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Representing the past in a compressed, efficient, and informative manner is a central problem for systems trained on sequential data. The HiPPO framework, originally proposed by Gu \u0026amp; Dao et al., provides a principled approach to sequential compression by projecting signals onto orthogonal polynomial (OP) bases via structured linear ordinary differential equations. Subsequent works have embedded these dynamics in state space models (SSMs), where HiPPO structure serves as an initialization. Nonlinear successors of these SSM methods such as Mamba are state-of-the-art for many tasks with long-range dependencies, but the mechanisms by which they represent and prioritize history remain largely implicit. In this work, we revisit the HiPPO framework with the goal of making these mechanisms explicit. We show how polynomial representations of history can be extended to support capabilities of modern SSMs such as adaptive memory allocation and associative memory, while retaining direct interpretability in the OP basis. We introduce a unified framework comprising five such extensions, which we collectively refer to as a \"HiPPO zoo.\" Each extension exposes a specific modeling capability through an explicit, interpretable modification of the HiPPO framework. The resulting models adapt their memory online and train in streaming settings with efficient updates. We illustrate the behaviors and modeling advantages of these extensions through a range of synthetic sequence modeling tasks, demonstrating that capabilities typically associated with modern SSMs can be realized through explicit, interpretable polynomial memory structures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 7 figures; to be published in ICML 2026; additional experimental results included"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21340","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:51Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-01T02:37:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianhui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaxiu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Huaxiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baolin","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Baolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GUI-Libra: Training Native GUI Agents to Reason and Act with Action-aware Supervision and Partially Verifiable RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:34:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-25T21:32:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:12:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-source native GUI agents still lag behind closed-source systems on long-horizon navigation tasks. This gap stems from two limitations: a shortage of high-quality, action-aligned reasoning data, and the direct adoption of generic post-training pipelines that overlook the unique challenges of GUI agents. We identify two fundamental issues in these pipelines: (i) standard SFT with CoT reasoning often hurts grounding, and (ii) step-wise RLVR-tyle training faces partial verifiability, where multiple actions can be correct but only a single demonstrated action is used for verification. This makes offline step-wise metrics weak predictors of online task success. In this work, we present GUI-Libra, a tailored training recipe that addresses these challenges. First, to mitigate the scarcity of action-aligned reasoning data, we introduce a data construction and filtering pipeline and release a curated 81K GUI reasoning dataset. Second, to reconcile reasoning with grounding, we propose action-aware SFT that mixes reasoning-then-action and direct-action data and reweights tokens to emphasize action and grounding. Third, to stabilize RL under partial verifiability, we identify the overlooked importance of KL regularization in RLVR and show that a KL trust region is critical for improving offline-to-online predictability; we further introduce success-adaptive scaling to downweight unreliable negative gradients. Across diverse web and mobile benchmarks, GUI-Libra consistently improves both step-wise accuracy and end-to-end task completion. Our results suggest that carefully designed post-training and data curation can unlock significantly stronger task-solving capabilities without costly online data collection. We release our dataset, code, and models to facilitate further research on data-efficient post-training for reasoning-capable GUI agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"57 pages, 17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:40:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21858","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21858","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21858","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dezhi","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Dezhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengzhao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zhengzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiliang","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Qiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haofei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changpeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Changpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Runyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ProactiveMobile: A Comprehensive Benchmark for Boosting Proactive Intelligence on Mobile Devices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:32:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T13:46:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:46:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-28T08:17:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:33:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-08T09:18:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:47:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have made significant progress in mobile agent development, yet their capabilities are predominantly confined to a reactive paradigm, where they merely execute explicit user commands. The emerging paradigm of proactive intelligence, where agents autonomously anticipate needs and initiate actions, represents the next frontier for mobile agents. However, its development is critically bottlenecked by the lack of benchmarks that can address real-world complexity and enable objective, executable evaluation. To overcome these challenges, we introduce ProactiveMobile, a comprehensive benchmark designed to systematically advance research in this domain. ProactiveMobile formalizes the proactive task as inferring latent user intent across four dimensions of on-device contextual signals and generating an executable function sequence from a comprehensive function pool of 63 APIs. The benchmark features over 3,660 instances of 14 scenarios that embrace real-world complexity through multi-answer annotations. To ensure quality, a team of 30 experts conducts a final audit of the benchmark, verifying factual accuracy, logical consistency, and action feasibility, and correcting any non-compliant entries. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our fine-tuned Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct achieves a success rate of 19.15%, outperforming o1 (15.71%) and GPT-5 (7.39%). This result indicates that proactivity is a critical competency widely lacking in current MLLMs, yet it is learnable, emphasizing the importance of the proposed benchmark for proactivity evaluation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21858","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-11T02:47:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21404","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21404","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21404","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanshan","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Shanshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Tino","name":"Tino, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Cooperation to Hierarchy: A Study of Dynamics of Hierarchy Emergence in a Multi-Agent System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:17:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:10:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-05T10:09:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T00:37:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A central premise in evolutionary biology is that individual variation can generate information asymmetries that facilitate the emergence of hierarchical organisation. To examine this process, we develop an agent-based model (ABM) to identify the minimal conditions under which hierarchy arises in dynamic multi-agent systems, focusing on the roles of initial heterogeneity and mutation amplitude across generations. Hierarchical organisation is quantified using the Trophic Incoherence (TI) metric, which captures directional asymmetries in interaction networks. Our results show that even small individual differences can be amplified through repeated local interactions involving reproduction, competition, and cooperation, but that hierarchical order is markedly more sensitive to mutation amplitude than to initial heterogeneity. Across repeated trials, stable hierarchies reliably emerge only when mutation amplitude is sufficiently high, while initial heterogeneity primarily affects early formation rather than long-term persistence. Overall, these findings demonstrate how simple interaction rules can give rise to both the emergence and persistence of hierarchical organisation, providing a quantitative account of how structured inequality can develop from initially homogeneous populations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 8 figures. This version includes a minor notation clarification in Eq. (11) by introducing the parameter beta in the softmax formulation. No other changes have been made"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21404","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:25Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-06T02:17:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21384","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21384","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21384","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick E.","familyName":"Farrell","name":"Farrell, Patrick E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josef","familyName":"Málek","name":"Málek, Josef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ondřej","familyName":"Souček","name":"Souček, Ondřej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umberto","familyName":"Zerbinati","name":"Zerbinati, Umberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A kinetic interpretation of thermomechanical restrictions of continua"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:27:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-02T22:20:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:45:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Rajagopal and Srinivasa's thermodynamic framework derives constitutive relations in continuum mechanics from two scalar functions describing energy storage and entropy production via a constrained optimization principle. In parallel, kinetic theory obtains constitutive laws through moment closure, most notably via the Chapman--Enskog expansion. This work has three objectives. First, we establish a connection between these approaches by providing a kinetic interpretation of the Rajagopal--Srinivasa principle of maximal entropy production, under appropriate albeit restrictive hypotheses. For a Bhatnagar--Gross--Krook-type approximation, we show that the Rajagopal--Srinivasa principle is equivalent to a minimal relaxation-time principle, selecting among admissible constitutive responses the one with the fastest compatible relaxation toward equilibrium. Second, we review the classical kinetic description of continua in a manner accessible to those familiar with continuum thermodynamics. Third, we propose a hybrid Chapman--Enskog--Rajagopal--Srinivasa approach which computes the thermodynamic relations and entropy production from the Chapman--Enskog expansion, and then invokes the Rajagopal--Srinivasa principle to determine the other constitutive relations. This recovers the standard Euler and Navier--Stokes--Fourier constitutive laws for monatomic gases. We also demonstrate how different choices of selection procedure can be more informative than the classical Chapman--Enskog closure in the context of an inviscid compressible Leslie--Ericksen model arising in liquid crystals."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In memory of Professor K. R. Rajagopal"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21384","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:55Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:07:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22135","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22135","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22135","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danel","familyName":"Ahman","name":"Ahman, Danel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrej","familyName":"Bauer","name":"Bauer, Andrej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sheaves as oracle computations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic (math.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.4.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"03B45, 03D75","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:28:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:06:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-28T20:02:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:07:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In type theory, an oracle may be specified abstractly by a predicate whose domain is the type of queries asked of the oracle, and whose proofs are the oracle answers. Such a specification induces an oracle modality that captures a computational intuition about oracles: at each step of reasoning we either know the result, or we ask the oracle a query and proceed upon receiving an answer. We characterize an oracle modality as the least one forcing the given predicate. We establish an adjoint retraction between modalities and propositional containers, from which it follows that every modality is an oracle modality. The left adjoint maps sums to suprema, which makes suprema of modalities easy to compute when they are given in terms of oracle modalities. We also study sheaves for oracle modalities. We describe sheafification in terms of a quotient-inductive type of computation trees, and describe sheaves as algebras for the corresponding monad. We also introduce equifoliate trees, an intensional notion of oracle computation given by a (non-propositional) container. Equifoliate trees descend to sheaves, and modally cover them. As an application, we give a concrete description of all Lawvere-Tierney topologies in a realizability topos, closely related to a game-theoretic characterization by Takayuki Kihara."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22135","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:42Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:24:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Germán T.","familyName":"Eizaguirre","name":"Eizaguirre, Germán T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars","familyName":"Tissen","name":"Tissen, Lars","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Sánchez-Artigas","name":"Sánchez-Artigas, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Both Ends Count! Just How Good are LLM Agents at \"Text-to-Big SQL\"?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:12:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:15:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T14:47:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:49:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T14:09:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:48:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-13T13:29:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:35:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3805621.3807640","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-SQL and Big Data are both extensively benchmarked fields, yet there is limited research that evaluates them jointly. In the real world, Text-to-SQL systems are often embedded with Big Data workflows, such as large-scale data processing or interactive data analytics. We refer to this as ``Text-to-Big SQL''. However, existing text-to-SQL benchmarks remain narrowly scoped and overlook the cost and performance implications that arise at scale. For instance, translation errors that are minor on small datasets lead to substantial cost and latency overheads as data scales, a relevant issue completely ignored by text-to-SQL metrics.\n In this paper, we overcome this overlooked challenge by introducing novel and representative metrics for evaluating Text-to-Big SQL. Our study focuses on production-level LLM agents, a database-agnostic system adaptable to diverse user needs. Via an extensive evaluation of frontier models, we show that text-to-SQL metrics are insufficient for Big Data. In contrast, our proposed text-to-Big SQL metrics accurately reflect execution efficiency, cost, and the impact of data scale. For example, GPT-4o compensates for roughly 7% lower accuracy than the top-performing later-generation models with up to a 12.16x speedup, while GPT-5.2 is more than twice as cost-effective as Gemini 3 Pro at large input scales."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:16Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:24:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21394","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21394","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21394","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehran","familyName":"Kafai","name":"Kafai, Mehran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Habas","name":"Habas, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MemoPhishAgent: Memory-Augmented Multi-Modal LLM Agent for Phishing URL Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:50:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:09:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:32:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:17:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T19:48:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:08:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Traditional phishing website detection relies on static heuristics or reference lists, which lag behind rapidly evolving attacks. While recent systems incorporate large language models (LLMs), they are still prompt-based, deterministic pipelines that underutilize reasoning capability. We present MemoPhishAgent (MPA), a memory-augmented multi-modal LLM agent that dynamically orchestrates phishing-specific tools and leverages episodic memories of past reasoning trajectories to guide decisions on recurring and novel threats. On two public datasets, MPA outperforms three state-of-the-art (SOTA) baselines, improving recall by 13.6%. To better reflect realistic, user-facing phishing detection performance, we further evaluate MPA on a benchmark of real-world suspicious URLs actively crawled from five social media platforms, where it improves recall by 20%. Detailed analysis shows episodic memory contributes up to 27% recall gain without introducing additional computational overhead. The ablation study confirms the necessity of the agent-based approach compared to prompt-based baselines and validates the effectiveness of our tool design. Finally, MPA is deployed in production, processing 60K targeted high-risk URLs weekly, and achieving 91.44% recall, providing proactive protection for millions of customers. Together, our results show that combining multi-modal reasoning with episodic memory yields robust phishing detection in realistic user-exposure settings. Our implementation is available at https://github.com/XuanChen-xc/MemoPhishAgent.git."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 Industry Track Camera Ready"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21394","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:10Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-23T02:26:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21228","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21228","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21228","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuancheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yuancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haihua","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Haihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ImpRIF: Stronger Implicit Reasoning Leads to Better Complex Instruction Following"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-04T07:50:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T12:48:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T02:08:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As applications of large language models (LLMs) become increasingly complex, the demand for robust complex instruction following capabilities is growing accordingly. We argue that a thorough understanding of the instruction itself, especially the latent reasoning structure embedded between the lines, is crucial for improving instruction following. Therefore we target complex instructions that involve implicit reasoning, intricate logical relations, and multi-constraint dependencies. We propose ImpRIF, a method to enhance LLMs' understanding of implicit reasoning instructions, thereby improving its ability to follow complex instructions. We formalize such instructions as verifiable reasoning graphs, enabling programmatic verification and graph-driven chain-of-thought reasoning. Based on this formulation, we synthesize large-scale single- and multi-turn data, propose fine-tuning with graph reasoning, and apply reinforcement learning to explicitly train models to reason along the graph. On five complex instruction following benchmarks, our models substantially outperform their base models. These results demonstrate that enhancing implicit reasoning capabilities can significantly improve complex instruction following."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21228","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:05Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:59:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangcong","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Fangcong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Howard","familyName":"Yen","name":"Yen, Howard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danqi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Danqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DySCO: Dynamic Attention-Scaling Decoding for Long-Context Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:21:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:08:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-16T15:31:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:58:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding and reasoning over long contexts is a crucial capability for language models (LMs). Although recent models support increasingly long context windows, their accuracy often deteriorates as input length grows. In practice, models often struggle to keep attention aligned with the most relevant context throughout decoding. In this work, we propose DYSCO, a novel decoding algorithm for improving long-context reasoning. DYSCO leverages retrieval heads--a subset of attention heads specialized for longcontext retrieval--to identify task-relevant tokens at each decoding step and explicitly up-weight them. By doing so, DYSCO dynamically adjusts attention during generation to better utilize relevant context. The method is training-free and can be applied directly to any off-the-shelf LMs. Across multiple instruction-tuned and reasoning models, DYSCO consistently improves performance on challenging long-context reasoning benchmarks, yielding relative gains of up to 25% on MRCR and LongBenchV2 at 128K context length with modest additional compute. Further analysis highlights the importance of both dynamic attention rescaling and retrievalhead guided selection for the effectiveness of the method, while providing interpretability insights into decoding-time attention behavior. Our code is available at https://github.com/princeton-pli/DySCO."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-17T02:21:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21484","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21484","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21484","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushen","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yushen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified Unsupervised and Sparsely-Supervised 3D Object Detection by Semantic Pseudo-Labeling and Prototype Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:26:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:15:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-12T08:42:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:57:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D object detection is essential for autonomous driving and robotic perception, yet its reliance on large-scale manually annotated data limits scalability and adaptability. To reduce annotation dependency, unsupervised and sparsely-supervised paradigms have emerged. However, they face intertwined challenges: low-quality pseudo-labels, unstable feature mining, and a lack of a unified training framework. This paper proposes SPL, a unified training framework for both unsupervised and sparsely-supervised 3D object detection via \\underline{S}emantic \\underline{P}seudo-labeling and prototype \\underline{L}earning. SPL first generates high-quality pseudo-labels by integrating image semantics, point cloud geometry, and temporal cues, producing both 3D bounding boxes for dense objects and 3D point labels for sparse ones. These pseudo-labels are not used directly but as probabilistic priors within a novel, multi-stage prototype learning strategy. This strategy stabilizes feature representation learning through memory-based initialization and momentum-based prototype updating, effectively mining features from both labeled and unlabeled data. Extensive experiments on KITTI and nuScenes datasets demonstrate that SPL significantly outperforms state-of-the-art methods in both settings. Our work provides a robust and generalizable solution for learning 3D object detectors with minimal or no manual annotations. Our code is available at https://github.com/TossherO/SPL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21484","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:22Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:25:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21697","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21697","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21697","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binhang","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Binhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhiyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin Song","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Jin Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EditFlow: Benchmarking and Optimizing Code Edit Recommendation Systems via Reconstruction of Developer Flows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:02:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:36:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-30T01:32:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:14:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-08T02:21:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-09T00:19:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3798249","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) for code editing have achieved remarkable progress, yet recent empirical studies reveal a fundamental disconnect between technical accuracy and developer productivity. Despite their strong benchmark performance, developers complete tasks 19% slower when using AI assistance, with over 68.81% of recommendations disrupting their mental flow. This misalignment stems from the use of static commit snapshots that lack temporal information, causing models to optimize for end results rather than the incremental, context-sensitive steps that align with developers' natural reasoning process.\n To bridge this gap, we present EditFlow, which benchmarks and optimizes subsequent code edit recommendation systems through the reconstruction of developer editing flows. EditFlow addresses three key challenges. First, collecting edit-order data that reflects developers' flow is inherently difficult: manual annotation introduces prohibitive overhead, while development logs capture only single trajectories instead of all plausible editing flows. Second, benchmarking recommendation performance against developers' ongoing editing flow requires a digital-twin-like simulation that can faithfully simulate the editing process. Third, existing heterogeneous systems vary drastically in scale and architecture, posing challenges for developing a unified optimization strategy that endows all models with mental-flow awareness regardless of design or capability.\n ......"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted to the Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages (OOPSLA 2026). (Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 10, OOPSLA1)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21697","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:56Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-09T02:24:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22013","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22013","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22013","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I-Hsiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, I-Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Wei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tse-Yu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Tse-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Chien","familyName":"Chiang","name":"Chiang, Yu-Chien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jen-Chien","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jen-Chien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Ting","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei-Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RobustVisRAG: Causality-Aware Vision-Based Retrieval-Augmented Generation under Visual Degradations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:27:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:58:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-05T07:12:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T01:36:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-08T06:31:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-10T01:05:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-26T08:59:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:39:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation (VisRAG) leverages vision-language models (VLMs) to jointly retrieve relevant visual documents and generate grounded answers based on multimodal evidence. However, existing VisRAG models degrade in performance when visual inputs suffer from distortions such as blur, noise, low light, or shadow, where semantic and degradation factors become entangled within pretrained visual encoders, leading to errors in both retrieval and generation stages. To address this limitation, we introduce RobustVisRAG, a causality-guided dual-path framework that improves VisRAG robustness while preserving efficiency and zero-shot generalization. RobustVisRAG uses a non-causal path to capture degradation signals through unidirectional attention and a causal path to learn purified semantics guided by these signals. Together with the proposed Non-Causal Distortion Modeling and Causal Semantic Alignment objectives, the framework enforces a clear separation between semantics and degradations, enabling stable retrieval and generation under challenging visual conditions. To evaluate robustness under realistic conditions, we introduce the Distortion-VisRAG dataset, a large-scale benchmark containing both synthetic and real-world degraded documents across seven domains, with 12 synthetic and 5 real distortion types that comprehensively reflect practical visual degradations. Experimental results show that RobustVisRAG improves retrieval, generation, and end-to-end performance by 7.35%, 6.35%, and 12.40%, respectively, on real-world degradations, while maintaining comparable accuracy on clean inputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR2026; Project Page: https://robustvisrag.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22013","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:42Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:25:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22095","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22095","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22095","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Doukas","name":"Doukas, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the emergence of quantum mechanics from stochastic processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:43:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:03:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-26T14:59:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-27T01:00:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The stochastic--quantum correspondence reinterprets quantum dynamics as arising from an underlying stochastic process on a configuration space. We generalize the correspondence by lifting an arbitrary stochastic kernel $Γ$ in finite dimension to a map $ϕ$ on $B(\\mathcal H)$, formulating the associated lift-compatibility relation, and giving an explicit dictionary between $Γ$ and CPTP (Kraus) maps. We isolate Chapman--Kolmogorov divisibility of the lifted family as the decisive additional constraint: when a CK-consistent CPTP family exists, the lift admits a Lindblad master equation form. In this picture, off-diagonal (phase) degrees of freedom act as a compressed carrier of history dependence not fixed by transition kernels alone; conversely, the apparent emergence of quantum phase information from a phase-blind stochastic description is explained as a memory effect. Finally, we state and prove a divisibility criterion for the underlying stochastic kernels, expressed as a condition involving divisibility of the lifted map together with a diagonality requirement on the density operator."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 1 figure. v2: corrected typos and expanded the discussion in Section 6"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22095","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:43Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:25:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21395","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21395","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21395","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongxin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Onur C.","familyName":"Koyun","name":"Koyun, Onur C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengjie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammet Fatih","familyName":"Demir","name":"Demir, Muhammet Fatih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Metin Nafi","familyName":"Gurcan","name":"Gurcan, Metin Nafi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Momentum Memory for Knowledge Distillation in Computational Pathology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:51:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:09:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-04T14:47:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-05T02:02:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-23T20:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:12:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal learning that integrates genomics and histopathology has shown strong potential in cancer diagnosis, yet its clinical translation is hindered by the limited availability of paired histology-genomics data. Knowledge distillation (KD) offers a practical solution by transferring genomic supervision into histopathology models, enabling accurate inference using histology alone. However, existing KD methods rely on batch-local alignment, which introduces instability due to limited within-batch comparisons and ultimately degrades performance.\n To address these limitations, we propose Momentum Memory Knowledge Distillation (MoMKD), a cross-modal distillation framework driven by a momentum-updated memory. This memory aggregates genomic and histopathology information across batches, effectively enlarging the supervisory context available to each mini-batch. Furthermore, we decouple the gradients of the genomics and histology branches, preventing genomic signals from dominating histology feature learning during training and eliminating the modality-gap issue at inference time.\n Extensive experiments on the TCGA-BRCA benchmark (HER2, PR, and ODX classification tasks) and an independent in-house testing dataset demonstrate that MoMKD consistently outperforms state-of-the-art MIL and multimodal KD baselines, delivering strong performance and generalization under histology-only inference. Overall, MoMKD establishes a robust and generalizable knowledge distillation paradigm for computational pathology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR 2026. Code: https://github.com/CAIR-LAB-WFUSM/MoMKD"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21395","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-25T02:36:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shufeng","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Shufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiduo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"I/O Optimizations for Graph-Based Disk-Resident Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search: A Design Space Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:02:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:20:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T09:36:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:42:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.14778/3801059.3801064","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search on SSD-backed indexes is increasingly I/O-bound (I/O accounts for 70--90\\% of query latency). We present an I/O-first framework for disk-based ANN that organizes techniques along three dimensions: memory layout, disk layout, and search algorithm. We introduce a page-level complexity model that explains how page locality and path length jointly determine page reads, and we validate the model empirically. Using consistent implementations across four public datasets, we quantify both single-factor effects and cross-dimensional synergies. We find that (i) memory-resident navigation and dynamic width provide the strongest standalone gains; (ii) page shuffle and page search are weak alone but complementary together; and (iii) a principled composition, OctopusANN, substantially reduces I/O and achieves 4.1--37.9\\% higher throughput than the state-of-the-art system Starling and 87.5--149.5\\% higher throughput than DiskANN at matched Recall@10=90\\%. Finally, we distill actionable guidelines for selecting storage-centric or hybrid designs across diverse concurrency levels and accuracy constraints, advocating systematic composition rather than isolated tweaks when pushing the performance frontier of disk-based ANN."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:24:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21424","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21424","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21424","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Galozy","name":"Galozy, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Structural Non-Preservation of Epistemic Behaviour under Policy Transformation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:55:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:11:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-20T01:13:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-23T00:20:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) agents under partial observability often condition actions on internally accumulated information such as memory or inferred latent context. We formalise such information-conditioned interaction patterns as behavioural dependency: variation in action selection with respect to internal information under fixed observations. This induces a probe-relative notion of $ε$-behavioural equivalence and a within-policy behavioural distance that quantifies probe sensitivity. We establish three structural results. First, the set of policies exhibiting non-trivial behavioural dependency is not closed under convex aggregation. Second, behavioural distance contracts under convex combination. Third, we prove a sufficient local condition under which gradient ascent on a skewed mixture objective decreases behavioural distance when a dominant-mode gradient aligns with the direction of steepest contraction. Minimal bandit and partially observable gridworld experiments provide controlled witnesses of these mechanisms. In the examined settings, behavioural distance decreases under convex aggregation and under continued optimisation with skewed latent priors, and in these experiments it precedes degradation under latent prior shift. These results identify structural conditions under which probe-conditioned behavioural separation is not preserved under common policy transformations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 3 figures. Under review at RLC 2026. Fixed references due to copy-paste errors"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21424","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-23T02:16:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21814","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21814","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21814","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heejin","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Heejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt Architecture Determines Reasoning Quality: A Variable Isolation Study on the Car Wash Problem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:40:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:46:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-19T16:28:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-20T01:13:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models consistently fail the \"car wash problem,\" a viral reasoning benchmark requiring implicit physical constraint inference. We present a variable isolation study (n=20 per condition, 6 conditions, 120 total trials) examining which prompt architecture layers in a production system enable correct reasoning. Using Claude 3.5 Sonnet with controlled hyperparameters (temperature 0.7, top_p 1.0), we find that the STAR (Situation-Task-Action-Result) reasoning framework alone raises accuracy from 0% to 85% (p=0.001, Fisher's exact test, odds ratio 13.22). Adding user profile context via vector database retrieval provides a further 10 percentage point gain, while RAG context contributes an additional 5 percentage points, achieving 100% accuracy in the full-stack condition. These results suggest that structured reasoning scaffolds -- specifically, forced goal articulation before inference -- matter substantially more than context injection for implicit constraint reasoning tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21814","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:49Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-20T02:42:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22077","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22077","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22077","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunggi","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Chunggi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayato","familyName":"Saiki","name":"Saiki, Hayato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tica","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Tica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eiji","familyName":"Ikeda","name":"Ikeda, Eiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenji","familyName":"Suzuki","name":"Suzuki, Kenji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhu-Tian","name":"Zhu-Tian, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanspeter","familyName":"Pfister","name":"Pfister, Hanspeter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ViSTAR: Virtual Skill Training with Augmented Reality with 3D Avatars and LLM coaching agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:26:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:02:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-18T15:08:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T01:20:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present ViSTAR, a Virtual Skill Training system in AR that supports self-guided basketball skill practice, with feedback on balance, posture, and timing. From a formative study with basketball players and coaches, the system addresses three challenges: understanding skills, identifying errors, and correcting mistakes. ViSTAR follows the Behavioral Skills Training (BST) framework-instruction, modeling, rehearsal, and feedback. It provides feedback through visual overlays, rhythm and timing cues, and an AI-powered coaching agent using 3D motion reconstruction. We generate verbal feedback by analyzing spatio-temporal joint data and mapping features to natural-language coaching cues via a Large Language Model (LLM). A key novelty is this feedback generation: motion features become concise coaching insights. In two studies (N=16), participants generally preferred our AI-generated feedback to coach feedback and reported that ViSTAR helped them notice posture and balance issues and refine movements beyond self-observation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22077","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:59:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21900","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21900","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21900","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingbin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jingbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huakang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Huakang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haohe","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haohe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binshen","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Binshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EmoOmni: Bridging Emotional Understanding and Expression in Omni-Modal LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:30:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:51:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T15:47:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:48:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The evolution of Omni-Modal Large Language Models~(Omni-LLMs) has revolutionized human--computer interaction, enabling unified audio-visual perception and speech response. However, existing Omni-LLMs struggle with complex real-world scenarios, often leading to superficial understanding and contextually mismatched emotional responses. This issue is further intensified by Omni-LLM's Thinker-Talker architectures, which are implicitly connected through hidden states, leading to the loss of emotional details. In this work, we present EmoOmni, a unified framework for accurate understanding and expression in multimodal emotional dialogue. At its core, we introduce the emotional Chain-of-Thought~(E-CoT), which enforces a reasoning from fine-grained multimodal perception to textual response. Moreover, we explicitly treat E-CoT as high-level emotional instructions that guide the talker, enabling accurate emotional expression. Complementing the model, we construct EmoOmniPipe to obtain the real-world annotated dialogue data and establish a benchmark, EmoOmniEval, to facilitate systematic assessment of multimodal emotional dialogue task. Experiments show that EmoOmni-7B achieves comparable performance with Qwen3Omni-30B-A3B-Thinking under the same talker."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21900","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:56Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:59:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21497","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21497","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21497","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yongchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangquan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Guangquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"See It, Say It, Sorted: An Iterative Training-Free Framework for Visually-Grounded Multimodal Reasoning in LVLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T02:13:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:18:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T07:38:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:34:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent large vision-language models (LVLMs) have demonstrated impressive reasoning ability by generating long chain-of-thought (CoT) responses. However, CoT reasoning in multimodal contexts is highly vulnerable to visual hallucination propagation: once an intermediate reasoning step becomes inconsistent with the visual evidence, subsequent steps-even if logically valid-can still lead to incorrect final answers. Existing solutions attempt to mitigate this issue by training models to \"think with images\" via reinforcement learning (RL). While effective, these methods are costly, model-specific, and difficult to generalize across architectures. Differently, we present a lightweight method that bypasses RL training and provides an iterative, training-free, plug-and-play framework for visually-grounded multimodal reasoning. Our key idea is to supervise each reasoning step at test time with visual evidence, ensuring that every decoded token is justified by corresponding visual cues. Concretely, we construct a textual visual-evidence pool that guides the model's reasoning generation. When existing evidence is insufficient, a visual decider module dynamically extracts additional relevant evidence from the image based on the ongoing reasoning context, expanding the pool until the model achieves sufficient visual certainty to terminate reasoning and produce the final answer. Extensive experiments on multiple LVLM backbones and benchmarks demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach. Our method achieves 16.5%-29.5% improvements on TreeBench and 13.7% RH-AUC gains on RH-Bench, substantially reducing hallucination rates while improving reasoning accuracy without additional training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR2026 Accepted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21497","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:59:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22136","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22136","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22136","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qunyou","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qunyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengbo","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Pengbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marina","familyName":"Zapater","name":"Zapater, Marina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Atienza","name":"Atienza, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SigmaQuant: Hardware-Aware Heterogeneous Quantization Method for Edge DNN Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:34:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:06:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T14:17:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:55:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep neural networks (DNNs) are essential for performing advanced tasks on edge or mobile devices, yet their deployment is often hindered by severe resource constraints, including limited memory, energy, and computational power. While uniform quantization provides a straightforward approach to compress model and reduce hardware requirement, it fails to fully leverage the varying robustness across layers, and often lead to accuracy degradation or suboptimal resource usage, particularly at low bitwidths. In contrast, heterogeneous quantization, which allocates different bitwidths to individual layers, can mitigate these drawbacks. Nonetheless, current heterogeneous quantization methods either needs huge brute-force design space search or lacks the adaptability to meet different hardware conditions, such as memory size, energy budget, and latency requirement. Filling these gaps, this work introduces \\textbf{\\textit{SigmaQuant}}, an adaptive layer-wise heterogeneous quantization framework designed to efficiently balance accuracy and resource usage for varied edge environments without exhaustive search."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22136","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21628","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21628","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21628","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longze","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Longze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ze","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Ze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jingpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyu","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Hengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ancheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Alinejad-Rokny","name":"Alinejad-Rokny, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RuCL: Stratified Rubric-Based Curriculum Learning for Multimodal Large Language Model Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:46:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T09:05:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:37:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has emerged as a prevailing paradigm for enhancing reasoning in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs). However, relying solely on outcome supervision risks reward hacking, where models learn spurious reasoning patterns to satisfy final answer checks. While recent rubric-based approaches offer fine-grained supervision signals, they suffer from high computational costs of instance-level generation and inefficient training dynamics caused by treating all rubrics as equally learnable. In this paper, we propose Stratified Rubric-based Curriculum Learning (RuCL), a novel framework that reformulates curriculum learning by shifting the focus from data selection to reward design. RuCL generates generalized rubrics for broad applicability and stratifies them based on the model's competence. By dynamically adjusting rubric weights during training, RuCL guides the model from mastering foundational perception to tackling advanced logical reasoning. Extensive experiments on various visual reasoning benchmarks show that RuCL yields a remarkable +7.83% average improvement over the Qwen2.5-VL-7B model, achieving a state-of-the-art accuracy of 60.06%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21628","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21626","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21626","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21626","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gholamreza","familyName":"Haffari","name":"Haffari, Gholamreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minxian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Minxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajkumar","familyName":"Buyya","name":"Buyya, Rajkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adel N.","familyName":"Toosi","name":"Toosi, Adel N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Layer Scheduling for MoE-Based LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:42:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T23:30:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:13:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success across a wide range of tasks, but serving them efficiently at scale remains a critical challenge due to their substantial computational and latency demands. While most existing inference frameworks rely on simple scheduling strategies such as First-Come-First-Serve (FCFS) at the engine level and Round-Robin (RR) at the scheduler or coordinator level, they often fail to fully utilize system resources and may suffer from issues such as head-of-line blocking and load imbalance. Recent advances in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models have also introduced new challenges in scheduling arising from expert parallelism and routing complexity. This research proposes a multi-layer scheduling framework tailored for MoE-based LLM serving. It targets scheduling at three levels: request-level, enginelevel, and expert-level. At the request level, we explore algorithms such as Shortest-Job-First (SJF) and priority-aware aging to improve throughput and reduce latency. At the engine level, we design load-aware dispatching strategies that account for the current prefix token load, KV cache utilization, and user stickiness to achieve better resource matching. At the expert level, we focus on alleviating expert hotspots and strategically placing inter-layer expert dependencies to balance load and improve routing efficiency. Extensive experimental results from more than 100 experiments conducted under diverse workload distributions show that our approach consistently outperforms the state-of-theart inference framework vLLM, achieving up to 17.8% reduction in Time To First Token (TTFT) latency and 13.3% reduction in Time-Per-Output-Token (TPOT) latency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21626","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:23:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21820","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21820","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21820","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peixia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peixia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenchen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiayu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongdong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pulak","familyName":"Purkait","name":"Purkait, Pulak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Joint Shadow Generation and Relighting via Light-Geometry Interaction Maps"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:47:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:46:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:20:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:31:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose Light-Geometry Interaction (LGI) maps, a novel representation that encodes light-aware occlusion from monocular depth. Unlike ray tracing, which requires full 3D reconstruction, LGI captures essential light-shadow interactions reliably and accurately, computed from off-the-shelf 2.5D depth map predictions. LGI explicitly ties illumination direction to geometry, providing a physics-inspired prior that constrains generative models. Without such prior, these models often produce floating shadows, inconsistent illumination, and implausible shadow geometry. Building on this representation, we propose a unified pipeline for joint shadow generation and relighting - unlike prior methods that treat them as disjoint tasks - capturing the intrinsic coupling of illumination and shadowing essential for modeling indirect effects. By embedding LGI into a bridge-matching generative backbone, we reduce ambiguity and enforce physically consistent light-shadow reasoning. To enable effective training, we curated the first large-scale benchmark dataset for joint shadow and relighting, covering reflections, transparency, and complex interreflections. Experiments show significant gains in realism and consistency across synthetic and real images. LGI thus bridges geometry-inspired rendering with generative modeling, enabling efficient, physically consistent shadow generation and relighting."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21820","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:55:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21333","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21333","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21333","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Pittaluga","name":"Pittaluga, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaid","familyName":"Tasneem","name":"Tasneem, Zaid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"You","name":"You, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manmohan","familyName":"Chandraker","name":"Chandraker, Manmohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyu","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HorizonForge: Driving Scene Editing with Any Trajectories and Any Vehicles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:03:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:05:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T00:13:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:55:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Controllable driving scene generation is critical for realistic and scalable autonomous driving simulation, yet existing approaches struggle to jointly achieve photorealism and precise control. We introduce HorizonForge, a unified framework that reconstructs scenes as editable Gaussian Splats and Meshes, enabling fine-grained 3D manipulation and language-driven vehicle insertion. Edits are rendered through a noise-aware video diffusion process that enforces spatial and temporal consistency, producing diverse scene variations in a single feed-forward pass without per-trajectory optimization. To standardize evaluation, we further propose HorizonSuite, a comprehensive benchmark spanning ego- and agent-level editing tasks such as trajectory modifications and object manipulation. Extensive experiments show that Gaussian-Mesh representation delivers substantially higher fidelity than alternative 3D representations, and that temporal priors from video diffusion are essential for coherent synthesis. Combining these findings, HorizonForge establishes a simple yet powerful paradigm for photorealistic, controllable driving simulation, achieving an 83.4% user-preference gain and a 25.19% FID improvement over the second best state-of-the-art method. Project page: https://horizonforge.github.io/ ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21333","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:55:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21778","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21778","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21778","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangbing","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Liangbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayak","familyName":"Paul","name":"Paul, Sayak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongsheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Elhoseiny","name":"Elhoseiny, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Statics to Dynamics: Physics-Aware Image Editing with Latent Transition Priors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:54:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:43:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T13:42:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:45:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Instruction-based image editing has achieved remarkable success in semantic alignment, yet state-of-the-art models frequently fail to render physically plausible results when editing involves complex causal dynamics, such as refraction or material deformation. We attribute this limitation to the dominant paradigm that treats editing as a discrete mapping between image pairs, which provides only boundary conditions and leaves transition dynamics underspecified. To address this, we reformulate physics-aware editing as predictive physical state transitions and introduce PhysicTran38K, a large-scale video-based dataset comprising 38K transition trajectories across five physical domains, constructed via a two-stage filtering and constraint-aware annotation pipeline. Building on this supervision, we propose PhysicEdit, an end-to-end framework equipped with a textual-visual dual-thinking mechanism. It combines a frozen Qwen2.5-VL for physically grounded reasoning with learnable transition queries that provide timestep-adaptive visual guidance to a diffusion backbone. Experiments show that PhysicEdit improves over Qwen-Image-Edit by 5.9% in physical realism and 10.1% in knowledge-grounded editing, setting a new state-of-the-art for open-source methods, while remaining competitive with leading proprietary models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"All code, checkpoints, and datasets are available at https://liangbingzhao.github.io/statics2dynamics/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21778","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:57Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21746","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21746","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21746","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abeer","familyName":"Dyoub","name":"Dyoub, Abeer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesca A.","familyName":"Lisi","name":"Lisi, Francesca A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"fEDM+: A Risk-Based Fuzzy Ethical Decision Making Framework with Principle-Level Explainability and Pluralistic Validation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:58:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:40:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T07:03:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:28:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In a previous work, we introduced the fuzzy Ethical Decision-Making framework (fEDM), a risk-based ethical reasoning architecture grounded in fuzzy logic. The original model combined a fuzzy Ethical Risk Assessment module (fERA) with ethical decision rules, enabled formal structural verification through Fuzzy Petri Nets (FPNs), and validated outputs against a single normative referent. Although this approach ensured formal soundness and decision consistency, it did not fully address two critical challenges: principled explainability of decisions and robustness under ethical pluralism. In this paper, we extend fEDM in two major directions. First, we introduce an Explainability and Traceability Module (ETM) that explicitly links each ethical decision rule to the underlying moral principles and computes a weighted principle-contribution profile for every recommended action. This enables transparent, auditable explanations that expose not only what decision was made but why, and on the basis of which principles. Second, we replace single-referent validation with a pluralistic semantic validation framework that evaluates decisions against multiple stakeholder referents, each encoding distinct principle priorities and risk tolerances. This shift allows principled disagreement to be formally represented rather than suppressed, thus increasing robustness and contextual sensitivity. The resulting extended fEDM, called fEDM+, preserves formal verifiability while achieving enhanced interpretability and stakeholder-aware validation, making it suitable as an oversight and governance layer for ethically sensitive AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"correcting captions of figures 7 and 8 and some other minor errors"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21746","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:09Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21515","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21515","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21515","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengrui","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Chengrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Lanzetti","name":"Lanzetti, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Mazumdar","name":"Mazumdar, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training Generalizable Collaborative Agents via Strategic Risk Aversion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:06:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:20:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T04:56:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:23:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many emerging agentic paradigms require agents to collaborate with one another (or people) to achieve shared goals. Unfortunately, existing approaches to learning policies for such collaborative problems produce brittle solutions that fail when paired with new partners. We attribute these failures to a combination of free-riding during training and a lack of strategic robustness. To address these problems, we study the concept of strategic risk aversion and interpret it as a principled inductive bias for generalizable cooperation with unseen partners. While strategically risk-averse players are robust to deviations in their partner's behavior by design, we show that, in collaborative games, they also (1) can have better equilibrium outcomes than those at classical game-theoretic concepts like Nash, and (2) exhibit less or no free-riding. Inspired by these insights, we develop a multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) algorithm that integrates strategic risk aversion into standard policy optimization methods. Our empirical results across collaborative benchmarks (including an LLM collaboration task) validate our theory and demonstrate that our approach consistently achieves reliable collaboration with heterogeneous and previously unseen partners across collaborative tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21515","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:08Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:11:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22208","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22208","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22208","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgy","familyName":"Savva","name":"Savva, Georgy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oscar","familyName":"Michel","name":"Michel, Oscar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daohan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Daohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suppakit","familyName":"Waiwitlikhit","name":"Waiwitlikhit, Suppakit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Meehan","name":"Meehan, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhairya","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Dhairya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srivats","familyName":"Poddar","name":"Poddar, Srivats","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saining","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Saining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solaris: Building a Multiplayer Video World Model in Minecraft"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:59:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:10:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T04:21:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:23:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing action-conditioned video generation models (video world models) are limited to single-agent perspectives, failing to capture the multi-agent interactions of real-world environments. We introduce Solaris, a multiplayer video world model that simulates consistent multi-view observations. To enable this, we develop a multiplayer data system designed for robust, continuous, and automated data collection on video games such as Minecraft. Unlike prior platforms built for single-player settings, our system supports coordinated multi-agent interaction and synchronized videos + actions capture. Using this system, we collect 12.64 million multiplayer frames and propose an evaluation framework for multiplayer movement, memory, grounding, building, and view consistency. We train Solaris using a staged pipeline that progressively transitions from single-player to multiplayer modeling, combining bidirectional, causal, and Self Forcing training. In the final stage, we introduce Checkpointed Self Forcing, a memory-efficient Self Forcing variant that enables a longer-horizon teacher. Results show our architecture and training design outperform existing baselines. Through open-sourcing our system and models, we hope to lay the groundwork for a new generation of multi-agent world models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project website: https://solaris-wm.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22208","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:32Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:20:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21743","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21743","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21743","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfeng","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Junfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinda","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jinda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangnan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xiangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Multi-Modal LLMs Reasoning via Difficulty-Aware Group Normalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:52:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:40:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T06:20:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:27:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) have significantly advanced the reasoning capabilities of large language models. Extending these methods to multimodal settings, however, faces a critical challenge: the instability of std-based normalization, which is easily distorted by extreme samples with nearly positive or negative rewards. Unlike pure-text LLMs, multimodal models are particularly sensitive to such distortions, as both perceptual and reasoning errors influence their responses. To address this, we characterize each sample by its difficulty, defined through perceptual complexity (measured via visual entropy) and reasoning uncertainty (captured by model confidence). Building on this characterization, we propose difficulty-aware group normalization (Durian), which re-groups samples by difficulty levels and shares the std within each group. Our approach preserves GRPO's intra-group distinctions while eliminating sensitivity to extreme cases, yielding significant performance gains across multiple multimodal reasoning benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21743","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:05Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:20:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21670","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21670","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21670","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomoya","familyName":"Kawabe","name":"Kawabe, Tomoya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rin","familyName":"Takano","name":"Takano, Rin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hierarchical LLM-Based Multi-Agent Framework with Prompt Optimization for Multi-Robot Task Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:08:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:33:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:28:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:17:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-robot task planning requires decomposing natural-language instructions into executable actions for heterogeneous robot teams. Conventional Planning Domain Definition Language (PDDL) planners provide rigorous guarantees but struggle to handle ambiguous or long-horizon missions, while large language models (LLMs) can interpret instructions and propose plans but may hallucinate or produce infeasible actions. We present a hierarchical multi-agent LLM-based planner with prompt optimization: an upper layer decomposes tasks and assigns them to lower-layer agents, which generate PDDL problems solved by a classical planner. When plans fail, the system applies TextGrad-inspired textual-gradient updates to optimize each agent's prompt and thereby improve planning accuracy. In addition, meta-prompts are learned and shared across agents within the same layer, enabling efficient prompt optimization in multi-agent settings. On the MAT-THOR benchmark, our planner achieves success rates of 0.95 on compound tasks, 0.84 on complex tasks, and 0.60 on vague tasks, improving over the previous state-of-the-art LaMMA-P by 2, 7, and 15 percentage points respectively. An ablation study shows that the hierarchical structure, prompt optimization, and meta-prompt sharing contribute roughly +59, +37, and +4 percentage points to the overall success rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA) 2026. 8 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21670","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:13Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:20:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22193","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22193","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22193","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Melody","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Melody","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Hewitt","name":"Hewitt, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Parametric Knowledge Access in Reasoning Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:43:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study reasoning for accessing world knowledge stored in a language model's parameters. For example, recalling that Canberra is Australia's capital may benefit from thinking through major cities and the concept of purpose-built capitals. While reasoning language models are trained via reinforcement learning to produce reasoning traces on tasks such as mathematics, they may not reason well for accessing their own world knowledge. We first find that models do not generate their best world knowledge reasoning by default: adding a simple \"think step-by-step\" cue demonstrates statistically significant improvement in knowledge recall but not math. Motivated by this, we propose training models to reason over their parametric knowledge using world-knowledge question answering as a verifiable reward. After reinforcement learning on TriviaQA (+9.9%), performance also improves on Natural Questions, HotpotQA, SimpleQA, and StrategyQA by 4.2%, 2.1%, 0.6%, and 3.0%, respectively. Reasoning models are under-optimized for parametric knowledge access, but can be easily trained to reason better."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22193","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:16:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:16:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:16:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sourav","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Sourav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dwaipayan","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Dwaipayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mandar","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Mandar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LiCQA : A Lightweight Complex Question Answering System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:28:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Over the last twenty years, significant progress has been made in designing and implementing Question Answering (QA) systems. However, addressing complex questions, the answers to which are spread across multiple documents, remains a challenging problem. Recent QA systems that are designed to handle complex questions work either on the basis of knowledge graphs, or utilise contem- porary neural models that are expensive to train, in terms of both computational resources and the volume of training data required. In this paper, we present LiCQA, an unsupervised question answer- ing model that works primarily on the basis of corpus evidence. We empirically compare the effectiveness and efficiency of LiCQA with two recently presented QA systems, which are based on different underlying principles. The results of our experiments show that LiCQA significantly outperforms these two state-of-the-art systems on benchmark data with noteworthy reduction in latency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:15:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22181","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22181","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22181","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter J.","familyName":"Cameron","name":"Cameron, Peter J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Around homogeneity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05D10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T18:28:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:09:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Forty-five years ago, a young researcher in finite permutation group theory encountered a paper by Robert Woodrow. The homogeneous triangle-free graph Woodrow described there seemed to be an infinite analogue of the Higman--Sims graph which had played an important role in the researcher's thesis. The encounter changed the course of the researcher's career. This paper is the story of that event and its aftermath.\n The final section of the paper suggests that Fra\\\"ıssé classes of rigid structures are a potentially interesting generalisation of Ramsey classes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In memory of Robert Woodrow"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22181","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:15:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:15:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:15:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22142","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22142","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22142","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sibei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Sibei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WeaveTime: Stream from Earlier Frames into Emergent Memory in VideoLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:45:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:06:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Multimodal Large Language Models have greatly improved visual understanding and reasoning, yet their quadratic attention and offline training protocols make them ill-suited for streaming settings where frames arrive sequentially and future observations are inaccessible. We diagnose a core limitation of current Video-LLMs, namely Time-Agnosticism, in which videos are treated as an unordered bag of evidence rather than a causally ordered sequence, yielding two failures in streams: temporal order ambiguity, in which the model cannot follow or reason over the correct chronological order, and past-current focus blindness where it fails to distinguish present observations from accumulated history. We present WeaveTime, a simple, efficient, and model agnostic framework that first teaches order and then uses order. We introduce a lightweight Temporal Reconstruction objective-our Streaming Order Perception enhancement-that instills order aware representations with minimal finetuning and no specialized streaming data. At inference, a Past-Current Dynamic Focus Cache performs uncertainty triggered, coarse-to-fine retrieval, expanding history only when needed. Plugged into exsiting Video-LLM without architectural changes, WeaveTime delivers consistent gains on representative streaming benchmarks, improving accuracy while reducing latency. These results establish WeaveTime as a practical path toward time aware stream Video-LLMs under strict online, time causal constraints. Code and weights will be made publicly available. Project Page: https://zhangyl4.github.io/publications/weavetime/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at CVPR 2026 (preview; camera-ready in preparation)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22142","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:53Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22125","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22125","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22125","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanmay","familyName":"Jayakumar","name":"Jayakumar, Thanmay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed Safi Ur Rahman","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Mohammed Safi Ur Rahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raj","familyName":"Dabre","name":"Dabre, Raj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ratish","familyName":"Puduppully","name":"Puduppully, Ratish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoop","familyName":"Kunchukuttan","name":"Kunchukuttan, Anoop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IndicIFEval: A Benchmark for Verifiable Instruction-Following Evaluation in 14 Indic Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:12:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:05:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Instruction-following benchmarks remain predominantly English-centric, leaving a critical evaluation gap for the hundreds of millions of Indic language speakers. We introduce IndicIFEval, a benchmark evaluating constrained generation of LLMs across 14 Indic languages using automatically verifiable, rule-based instructions. It comprises around 800 human-verified examples per language spread across two complementary subsets: IndicIFEval-Ground, translated prompts from IFEval (Zhou et al., 2023) carefully localized for Indic contexts, and IndicIFEval-Ground, synthetically generated instructions grounded in native Indic content. We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of major open-weight and proprietary models spanning both reasoning and non-reasoning models. While models maintain strong adherence to formatting constraints, they struggle significantly with lexical and cross-lingual tasks -- and despite progress in high-resource languages, instruction-following across the broader Indic family lags significantly behind English. We release IndicIFEval and its evaluation scripts to support progress on multilingual constrained generation (http://github.com/ai4bharat/IndicIFEval)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages + Appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22125","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:27Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22121","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22121","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22121","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Balint","familyName":"Pato","name":"Pato, Balint","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natalie","familyName":"Klco","name":"Klco, Natalie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trade-offs in Gauss's law error correction for lattice gauge theory quantum simulations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Lattice (hep-lat)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T17:09:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:05:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Gauss's law-based quantum error correction (GLQEC) offers a promising approach to reducing qubit overhead in lattice gauge theory simulations by leveraging built-in symmetries. For applications of GLQEC to 1+1D lattice quantum electrodynamics (QED), we identify two significant trade-offs. First, we prove via dimension-counting arguments that GLQEC requires periodic electric fields, thereby constraining the design space for lattice QED simulations. Second, we numerically compare GLQEC with a universal quantum error correction (UQEC) code, specifically the $d=3$ bitflip repetition code, and find that while GLQEC can achieve lower logical error rates in single-round error correction, it exhibits faster decoherence to the steady-state mixed ensemble under multiple rounds. The mixing speed penalty is manifest in observables of interest for both memory experiments and Hamiltonian evolution. We identify a mixing speed threshold, $p_{th}=0.277(2)$, above which using GLQEC exhibits even faster decoherence than without error correction. Our results highlight fundamental limitations of symmetry-based error correction schemes and inform corresponding constraints on formulations of lattice gauge theories compatible with error-robust quantum simulation techniques."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22121","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:14:22Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:14:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:14:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22075","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22075","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22075","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Drodt","name":"Drodt, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reiner","familyName":"Hähnle","name":"Hähnle, Reiner","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RustyDL: A Program Logic for Rust"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68Q60","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:25:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:02:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Rust is a modern programming language that guarantees memory safety and the absence of data races with a strong type system. We present RustyDL, a program logic for Rust, as a foundation for an auto-interactive, deductive verification tool for Rust. RustyDL reasons about Rust programs directly on the source code level, in contrast to other tools that are all based on translation to an intermediate language. A source-level program logic for Rust is crucial for a human-in-the-loop (HIL) style of verification that permits proving highly complex functional properties. We discuss specific Rust challenges in designing a program logic and calculus for HIL-style verification and propose a solution in each case. We provide a proof-of-concept of our ideas in the form of a prototype of a Rust instance of the deductive verification tool KeY."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Long version of paper published at 27th International Symposium on Formal Methods (FM 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22075","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22072","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22072","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22072","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Nickel","name":"Nickel, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Schrewe","name":"Schrewe, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Mai","name":"Mai, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucie","familyName":"Flek","name":"Flek, Lucie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding Artificial Theory of Mind: Perturbed Tasks and Reasoning in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:24:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:02:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Theory of Mind (ToM) refers to an agent's ability to model the internal states of others. Contributing to the debate whether large language models (LLMs) exhibit genuine ToM capabilities, our study investigates their ToM robustness using perturbations on false-belief tasks and examines the potential of Chain-of-Thought prompting (CoT) to enhance performance and explain the LLM's decision. We introduce a handcrafted, richly annotated ToM dataset, including classic and perturbed false belief tasks, the corresponding spaces of valid reasoning chains for correct task completion, subsequent reasoning faithfulness, task solutions, and propose metrics to evaluate reasoning chain correctness and to what extent final answers are faithful to reasoning traces of the generated CoT. We show a steep drop in ToM capabilities under task perturbation for all evaluated LLMs, questioning the notion of any robust form of ToM being present. While CoT prompting improves the ToM performance overall in a faithful manner, it surprisingly degrades accuracy for some perturbation classes, indicating that selective application is necessary."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22072","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:10Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22070","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.22070","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.22070","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica Y.","familyName":"Bo","name":"Bo, Jessica Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lillio","familyName":"Mok","name":"Mok, Lillio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashton","familyName":"Anderson","name":"Anderson, Ashton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language Models Exhibit Inconsistent Biases Towards Algorithmic Agents and Human Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T16:18:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T02:01:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are increasingly used in decision-making tasks that require them to process information from a variety of sources, including both human experts and other algorithmic agents. How do LLMs weigh the information provided by these different sources? We consider the well-studied phenomenon of algorithm aversion, in which human decision-makers exhibit bias against predictions from algorithms. Drawing upon experimental paradigms from behavioural economics, we evaluate how eightdifferent LLMs delegate decision-making tasks when the delegatee is framed as a human expert or an algorithmic agent. To be inclusive of different evaluation formats, we conduct our study with two task presentations: stated preferences, modeled through direct queries about trust towards either agent, and revealed preferences, modeled through providing in-context examples of the performance of both agents. When prompted to rate the trustworthiness of human experts and algorithms across diverse tasks, LLMs give higher ratings to the human expert, which correlates with prior results from human respondents. However, when shown the performance of a human expert and an algorithm and asked to place an incentivized bet between the two, LLMs disproportionately choose the algorithm, even when it performs demonstrably worse. These discrepant results suggest that LLMs may encode inconsistent biases towards humans and algorithms, which need to be carefully considered when they are deployed in high-stakes scenarios. Furthermore, we discuss the sensitivity of LLMs to task presentation formats that should be broadly scrutinized in evaluation robustness for AI safety."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Second Conference of the International Association for Safe and Ethical Artificial Intelligence (IASEAI 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22070","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:13:06Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:13:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:13:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21997","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21997","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21997","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"WeiZhe","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, WeiZhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanxin","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Fanxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing LLM-Based Test Generation by Eliminating Covered Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:16:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated test generation is essential for software quality assurance, with coverage rate serving as a key metric to ensure thorough testing. Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise in improving test generation, particularly in achieving higher coverage. However, while existing LLM-based test generation solutions perform well on small, isolated code snippets, they struggle when applied to complex methods under test. To address these issues, we propose a scalable LLM-based unit test generation method. Our approach consists of two key steps. The first step is context information retrieval, which uses both LLMs and static analysis to gather relevant contextual information associated with the complex methods under test. The second step, iterative test generation with code elimination, repeatedly generates unit tests for the code slice, tracks the achieved coverage, and selectively removes code segments that have already been covered. This process simplifies the testing task and mitigates issues arising from token limits or reduced reasoning effectiveness associated with excessively long contexts. Through comprehensive evaluations on open-source projects, our approach outperforms state-of-the-art LLM-based and search-based methods, demonstrating its effectiveness in achieving high coverage on complex methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 4 figures, supplementary material included"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21997","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:18Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21992","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21992","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21992","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekai","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PanoEnv: Exploring 3D Spatial Intelligence in Panoramic Environments with Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:12:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:57:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"360 panoramic images are increasingly used in virtual reality, autonomous driving, and robotics for holistic scene understanding. However, current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) struggle with 3D spatial reasoning on Equirectangular Projection (ERP) images due to geometric distortion and limited 3D supervision. We introduce PanoEnv, a large-scale VQA benchmark built from synthetic 3D environments, containing 14.8K questions across five categories (e.g., relative position, volume comparison) grounded in accurate 3D annotations including depth, segmentation, and bounding boxes. Benchmarking 14 state-of-the-art VLMs reveals limited 3D understanding, achieving only 49.34% overall accuracy and 8.36% on open-ended (OE) questions. To enhance 3D reasoning, we propose a reinforcement learning post-training framework based on Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with a ground-truth-guided reward that incorporates five geometry-aware strategies such as distance tolerance and spatial consistency. A two-stage curriculum further mitigates catastrophic forgetting: Stage 1 trains on structured tasks (true/false and multiple choice), and Stage 2 fine-tunes on mixed open-ended data to improve generalization. Our 7B model achieves new state-of-the-art performance, improving overall accuracy to 52.93% (+3.59%) and open-ended accuracy to 14.83% while maintaining structured-task performance. It also achieves top semantic evaluation scores (Q-Score 6.24, P-Score 5.95), surpassing 32B models. These results demonstrate that PanoEnv-QA and our curriculum-based RL framework effectively instill 3D spatial intelligence in VLMs for omnidirectional perception."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21992","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21986","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21986","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21986","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suman","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Suman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Priyanka","familyName":"Sahu","name":"Sahu, Priyanka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhabrata","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Subhabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sameer Kumar","familyName":"Mallik","name":"Mallik, Sameer Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susmita","familyName":"Jana","name":"Jana, Susmita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alok","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Alok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Himadri Nandan","familyName":"Mohanty","name":"Mohanty, Himadri Nandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaushik","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Kaushik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B. R. K.","familyName":"Nanda","name":"Nanda, B. R. K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satyaprakash","familyName":"Sahoo","name":"Sahoo, Satyaprakash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum Resistance in Multilayer Graphene-BiFeO3 Memristor for Brain-Inspired Computing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:04:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:56:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the era of big data and the Internet of Things, quantum-level control of conductance states offers a promising route toward high-density data storage and brain-inspired neuromorphic computing. Although quantum conductance (QC) phenomena have been demonstrated in various metal oxide memristors, achieving reliable and precise control over quantized states remains in its infancy. Here, we demonstrate bidirectional quantum conductance states in multifunctional BiFeO3 (BFO) perovskite memristors integrated with multilayer-graphene contacts, enabling higher-order tunability and revealing the potential of perovskite-2D heterostructures for quantum-engineered memory and computing devices. XPS analysis provides detailed insights into oxygen vacancy dynamics in BFO, whereas first-principles density functional theory calculations clearly reveal a strong localized electric field at the graphene-BFO interface. Our devices exhibit current-controlled higher-order QC transitions facilitated by quantum point contact formation, giving rise to quantized conductance states during both SET and RESET processes. Time-lag correlation maps quantify the stochastic evolution of QC states under dynamic voltage-pulse tuning schemes. Notably, the quantized conductance states effectively emulate synaptic potentiation and depression, enabling precise weight modulation for high-accuracy image and digit recognition in convolutional neural networks. These findings establish perovskite-2D heterostructures as promising candidates for QC-driven resistive switching and demonstrate their potential for developing controllable quantum memristors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21986","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:11:02Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:11:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:11:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21983","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21983","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21983","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingchao","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jingchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkai","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Jingkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiao","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yuxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingcheng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jingcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangrui","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Xiangrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhouping","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Zhouping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Humanizing Robot Gaze Shifts: A Framework for Natural Gaze Shifts in Humanoid Robots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T15:02:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:56:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Leveraging auditory and visual feedback for attention reorientation is essential for natural gaze shifts in social interaction. However, enabling humanoid robots to perform natural and context-appropriate gaze shifts in unconstrained human--robot interaction (HRI) remains challenging, as it requires the coupling of cognitive attention mechanisms and biomimetic motion generation. In this work, we propose the Robot Gaze-Shift (RGS) framework, which integrates these two components into a unified pipeline. First, RGS employs a vision--language model (VLM)-based gaze reasoning pipeline to infer context-appropriate gaze targets from multimodal interaction cues, ensuring consistency with human gaze-orienting regularities. Second, RGS introduces a conditional Vector Quantized-Variational Autoencoder (VQ-VAE) model for eye--head coordinated gaze-shift motion generation, producing diverse and human-like gaze-shift behaviors. Experiments validate that RGS effectively replicates human-like target selection and generates realistic, diverse gaze-shift motions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"submitted to AIM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21983","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21974","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21974","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21974","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martina","familyName":"Iannacito","name":"Iannacito, Martina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Piccinini","name":"Piccinini, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valeria","familyName":"Simoncini","name":"Simoncini, Valeria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Subspace gradient descent method for linear tensor equations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"G.1.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"65F10, 65F99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:54:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:56:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The numerical solution of algebraic tensor equations is a largely open and challenging task. Assuming that the operator is symmetric and positive definite, we propose two new gradient-descent type methods for tensor equations that generalize the recently proposed Subspace Conjugate Gradient (SS-CG), D. Palitta et al, SIAM J. Matrix Analysis and Appl (2025). As our interest is mainly in a modest number of tensor modes, the Tucker format is used to efficiently represent low-rank tensors. Moreover, mixed-precision strategies are employed in certain subtasks to improve the memory usage, and different preconditioners are applied to enhance convergence. The potential of our strategies is illustrated by experimental results on tensor-oriented discretizations of three-dimensional partial differential equations with separable coefficients. Comparisons with the state-of-the-art Alternating Minimal Energy (AMEn) algorithm confirm the competitiveness of the proposed strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 2 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21974","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21965","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21965","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21965","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Margaryan","name":"Margaryan, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Hamelryck","name":"Hamelryck, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compact Circulant Layers with Spectral Priors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:48:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Critical applications in areas such as medicine, robotics and autonomous systems require compact (i.e., memory efficient), uncertainty-aware neural networks suitable for edge and other resource-constrained deployments. We study compact spectral circulant and block-circulant-with-circulant-blocks (BCCB) layers: FFT-diagonalizable circular convolutions whose weights live directly in the real FFT (RFFT) half (1D) or half-plane (2D). Parameterizing filters in the frequency domain lets us impose simple spectral structure, perform structured variational inference in a low-dimensional weight space, and calculate exact layer spectral norms, enabling inexpensive global Lipschitz bounds and margin-based robustness diagnostics. By placing independent complex Gaussians on the Hermitian support we obtain a discrete instance of the spectral representation of stationary kernels, inducing an exact stationary Gaussian-process prior over filters on the discrete circle/torus. We exploit this to define a practical spectral prior and a Hermitian-aware low-rank-plus-diagonal variational posterior in real coordinates. Empirically, spectral circulant/BCCB layers are effective compact building blocks in both (variational) Bayesian and point estimate regimes: compact Bayesian neural networks on MNIST-\u0026gt;Fashion-MNIST, variational heads on frozen CIFAR-10 features, and deterministic ViT projections on CIFAR-10/Tiny ImageNet; spectral layers match strong baselines while using substantially fewer parameters and with tighter Lipschitz certificates."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21965","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21963","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21963","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21963","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgos","familyName":"Tolias","name":"Tolias, Giorgos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiri","familyName":"Matas","name":"Matas, Jiri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Barath","name":"Barath, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Global-Aware Edge Prioritization for Pose Graph Initialization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:44:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The pose graph is a core component of Structure-from-Motion (SfM), where images act as nodes and edges encode relative poses. Since geometric verification is expensive, SfM pipelines restrict the pose graph to a sparse set of candidate edges, making initialization critical. Existing methods rely on image retrieval to connect each image to its $k$ nearest neighbors, treating pairs independently and ignoring global consistency. We address this limitation through the concept of edge prioritization, ranking candidate edges by their utility for SfM. Our approach has three components: (1) a GNN trained with SfM-derived supervision to predict globally consistent edge reliability; (2) multi-minimal-spanning-tree-based pose graph construction guided by these ranks; and (3) connectivity-aware score modulation that reinforces weak regions and reduces graph diameter. This globally informed initialization yields more reliable and compact pose graphs, improving reconstruction accuracy in sparse and high-speed settings and outperforming SOTA retrieval methods on ambiguous scenes. The ode and trained models are available at https://github.com/weitong8591/global_edge_prior."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted to CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21963","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:28Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21962","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21962","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21962","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng-Yun","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Meng-Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixiang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zhixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-Long","familyName":"You","name":"You, Wen-Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tighter thermalization bounds for perturbed quantum many-body scars"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:44:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/2hjm-m1v9","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum many-body scars (QMBS) are exceptional eigenstates that defy thermalization, enabling long-lived coherent dynamics in strongly interacting systems. However, their stability under perturbations remains inadequately understood. In this work, we derive improved lower bounds on the thermalization time of QMBS under local perturbations with strength $λ$. Using both numerical simulations and analytical reasoning, we show that exact QMBS exhibit slow thermalization, with a timescale scaling as $τ\\sim \\mathcal{O}(λ^{-1/d})$ owing to the stabilizing restricted spectrum-generating algebra (RSGA), which is a significant improvement over previous bounds (e.g., $τ\\sim \\mathcal{O}(λ^{-1/(d+1)})$). Counterintuitively, approximate QMBS can thermalize even more slowly under generic perturbations, exhibiting $τ\\sim \\mathcal{O}(λ^{-2})$ scaling due to second-order perturbative effects in the absence of such protective structure. These distinct thermalization behaviors clarify how exact and approximate scars maintain coherence. Our work advances previous findings by establishing a tighter bound on the thermalization time, clarifying when scarred dynamics remain long-lived under weak but generic perturbations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21962","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:27Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchun","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Yuchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingli","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Bingli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Collaborate via Structures: Cluster-Guided Item Alignment for Federated Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:39:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated recommendation facilitates collaborative model training across distributed clients while keeping sensitive user interaction data local. Conventional approaches typically rely on synchronizing high-dimensional item representations between the server and clients. This paradigm implicitly assumes that precise geometric alignment of embedding coordinates is necessary for collaboration across clients. We posit that establishing relative semantic relationships among items is more effective than enforcing shared representations. Specifically, global semantic relations serve as structural constraints for items. Within these constraints, the framework allows item representations to vary locally on each client, which flexibility enables the model to capture fine-grained user personalization while maintaining global consistency. To this end, we propose Cluster-Guided FedRec framework (CGFedRec), a framework that transforms uploaded embeddings into compact cluster labels. In this framework, the server functions as a global structure discoverer to learn item clusters and distributes only the resulting labels. This mechanism explicitly cuts off the downstream transmission of item embeddings, relieving clients from maintaining global shared item embeddings. Consequently, CGFedRec achieves the effective injection of global collaborative signals into local item representations without transmitting full embeddings. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach significantly improves communication efficiency while maintaining superior recommendation accuracy across multiple datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21952","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21952","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21952","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujian","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changjie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Changjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linzhe","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Linzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Sijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MindDriver: Introducing Progressive Multimodal Reasoning for Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:34:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:55:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLM) exhibit strong reasoning capabilities, showing promise for end-to-end autonomous driving systems. Chain-of-Thought (CoT), as VLM's widely used reasoning strategy, is facing critical challenges. Existing textual CoT has a large gap between text semantic space and trajectory physical space. Although the recent approach utilizes future image to replace text as CoT process, it lacks clear planning-oriented objective guidance to generate images with accurate scene evolution. To address these, we innovatively propose MindDriver, a progressive multimodal reasoning framework that enables VLM to imitate human-like progressive thinking for autonomous driving. MindDriver presents semantic understanding, semantic-to-physical space imagination, and physical-space trajectory planning. To achieve aligned reasoning processes in MindDriver, we develop a feedback-guided automatic data annotation pipeline to generate aligned multimodal reasoning training data. Furthermore, we develop a progressive reinforcement fine-tuning method to optimize the alignment through progressive high- level reward-based learning. MindDriver demonstrates superior performance in both nuScences open-loop and Bench2Drive closed-loop evaluation. Codes are available at https://github.com/hotdogcheesewhite/MindDriver."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR2026; Yujian Yuan and Lingjun Zhang contributed equally with random order"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21952","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21951","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21951","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21951","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luoyi","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Luoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinbing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RADAR: Reasoning as Discrimination with Aligned Representations for LLM-based Knowledge Graph Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:34:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:54:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge graph reasoning (KGR) infers missing facts, with recent advances increasingly harnessing the semantic priors and reasoning abilities of Large Language Models (LLMs). However, prevailing generative paradigms are prone to memorizing surface-level co-occurrences rather than learning genuine relational semantics, limiting out-of-distribution generalization. To address this, we propose RADAR, which reformulates KGR from generative pattern matching to discriminative relational reasoning. We recast KGR as discriminative entity selection, where reinforcement learning enforces relative entity separability beyond token-likelihood imitation. Leveraging this separability, inference operates directly in representation space, ensuring consistency with the discriminative optimization and bypassing generation-induced hallucinations. Across four benchmarks, RADAR achieves 5-6% relative gains on link prediction and triple classification over strong LLM baselines, while increasing task-relevant mutual information in intermediate representations by 62.9%, indicating more robust and transferable relational reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21951","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:10:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:10:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:10:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21929","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21929","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21929","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"JiaKui","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, JiaKui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jialun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liying","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Liying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanye","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yanye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geometry-as-context: Modulating Explicit 3D in Scene-consistent Video Generation to Geometry Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T14:09:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:53:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scene-consistent video generation aims to create videos that explore 3D scenes based on a camera trajectory. Previous methods rely on video generation models with external memory for consistency, or iterative 3D reconstruction and inpainting, which accumulate errors during inference due to incorrect intermediary outputs, non-differentiable processes, and separate models. To overcome these limitations, we introduce ``geometry-as-context\". It iteratively completes the following steps using an autoregressive camera-controlled video generation model: (1) estimates the geometry of the current view necessary for 3D reconstruction, and (2) simulates and restores novel view images rendered by the 3D scene. Under this multi-task framework, we develop the camera gated attention module to enhance the model's capability to effectively leverage camera poses. During the training phase, text contexts are utilized to ascertain whether geometric or RGB images should be generated. To ensure that the model can generate RGB-only outputs during inference, the geometry context is randomly dropped from the interleaved text-image-geometry training sequence. The method has been tested on scene video generation with one-direction and forth-and-back trajectories. The results show its superiority over previous approaches in maintaining scene consistency and camera control."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21929","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:09:38Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:09:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:09:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21898","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21898","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21898","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michal","familyName":"Botur","name":"Botur, Michal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kruml","name":"Kruml, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Paseka","name":"Paseka, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantales carrying ortholattice structure"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic (math.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"06F07, 06C15, 03G12, 03G25","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:28:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:51:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates the intersection of residuated structures from many-valued logic and orthomodular lattices from quantum logic. We explore whether non-Boolean structures can simultaneously satisfy residuation principles and orthocomplementation requirements. Our main contribution is a study of Girard posets with inversions, providing a characterization theorem where a unital residuated poset is Girard if and only if it admits an inversion satisfying specific adjointness conditions. We prove that any complemented lattice admitting an integral residuated structure must be Boolean, which motivates our search for orthomodular examples in the non-integral case. We answer this by demonstrating that the lattice $C(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ of closed subspaces of $n$-dimensional real coordinate space carries both an orthomodular and a commutative Girard quantale structure. This construction provides a concrete non-Boolean framework unifying quantum-logical and many-valued logical reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21898","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:53Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21887","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21887","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21887","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changjiang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Changjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixian","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaichen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jixing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujian","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ExpLang: Improved Exploration and Exploitation in LLM Reasoning with On-Policy Thinking Language Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T13:10:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:50:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current large reasoning models (LRMs) have shown strong ability on challenging tasks after reinforcement learning (RL) based post-training. However, previous work mainly focuses on English reasoning in expectation of the strongest performance, despite the demonstrated potential advantage of multilingual thinking, as well as the requirement for native thinking traces by global users. In this paper, we propose ExpLang, a novel LLM post-training pipeline that enables on-policy thinking language selection to improve exploration and exploitation during RL with the use of multiple languages. The results show that our method steadily outperforms English-only training with the same training budget, while showing high thinking language compliance for both seen and unseen languages. Analysis shows that, by enabling on-policy thinking language selection as an action during RL, ExpLang effectively extends the RL exploration space with diversified language preference and improves the RL exploitation outcome with leveraged non-English advantage. The method is orthogonal to most RL algorithms and opens up a new perspective on using multilinguality to improve LRMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21887","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:37Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia Cheng","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Chia Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An-Zi","familyName":"Yen","name":"Yen, An-Zi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hen-Hsen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Hen-Hsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsin-Hsi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hsin-Hsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Personalized Graph-Empowered Large Language Model for Proactive Information Access"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:43:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:49:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Since individuals may struggle to recall all life details and often confuse events, establishing a system to assist users in recalling forgotten experiences is essential. While numerous studies have proposed memory recall systems, these primarily rely on deep learning techniques that require extensive training and often face data scarcity due to the limited availability of personal lifelogs. As lifelogs grow over time, systems must also adapt quickly to newly accumulated data. Recently, large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across various tasks, making them promising for personalized applications. In this work, we present a framework that leverages LLMs for proactive information access, integrating personal knowledge graphs to enhance the detection of access needs through a refined decision-making process. Our framework offers high flexibility, enabling the replacement of base models and the modification of fact retrieval methods for continuous improvement. Experimental results demonstrate that our approach effectively identifies forgotten events, supporting users in recalling past experiences more efficiently."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:08:00Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:08:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:08:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21857","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21857","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21857","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jabez","familyName":"Magomere","name":"Magomere, Jabez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Kochkina","name":"Kochkina, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Mensah","name":"Mensah, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simerjot","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Simerjot","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fernando","familyName":"Acero","name":"Acero, Fernando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arturo","familyName":"Oncevay","name":"Oncevay, Arturo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charese H.","familyName":"Smiley","name":"Smiley, Charese H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaomo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuela","familyName":"Veloso","name":"Veloso, Manuela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distill and Align Decomposition for Enhanced Claim Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:32:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Complex claim verification requires decomposing sentences into verifiable subclaims, yet existing methods struggle to align decomposition quality with verification performance. We propose a reinforcement learning (RL) approach that jointly optimizes decomposition quality and verifier alignment using Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO). Our method integrates: (i) structured sequential reasoning; (ii) supervised finetuning on teacher-distilled exemplars; and (iii) a multi-objective reward balancing format compliance, verifier alignment, and decomposition quality. Across six evaluation settings, our trained 8B decomposer improves downstream verification performance to (71.75%) macro-F1, outperforming prompt-based approaches ((+1.99), (+6.24)) and existing RL methods ((+5.84)). Human evaluation confirms the high quality of the generated subclaims. Our framework enables smaller language models to achieve state-of-the-art claim verification by jointly optimising for verification accuracy and decomposition quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EACL Findings 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21857","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:53Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21854","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21854","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21854","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustafa","familyName":"Dogan","name":"Dogan, Mustafa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilker","familyName":"Kesen","name":"Kesen, Ilker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iacer","familyName":"Calixto","name":"Calixto, Iacer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aykut","familyName":"Erdem","name":"Erdem, Aykut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erkut","familyName":"Erdem","name":"Erdem, Erkut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FewMMBench: A Benchmark for Multimodal Few-Shot Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T12:30:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:48:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As multimodal large language models (MLLMs) advance in handling interleaved image-text data, assessing their few-shot learning capabilities remains an open challenge. In this paper, we introduce FewMMBench, a comprehensive benchmark designed to evaluate MLLMs under few-shot conditions, with a focus on In-Context Learning (ICL) and Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting. Covering a diverse suite of multimodal understanding tasks, from attribute recognition to temporal reasoning, FewMMBench enables systematic analysis across task types, model families, and prompting strategies. We evaluate 26 open-weight MLLMs from six model families across zero-shot, few-shot, and CoT-augmented few-shot settings. Our findings reveal that instruction-tuned models exhibit strong zero-shot performance but benefit minimally, or even regress, with additional demonstrations or CoT reasoning. Retrieval-based demonstrations and increased context size also yield limited gains. These results highlight FewMMBench as a rigorous testbed for diagnosing and advancing few-shot capabilities in multimodal LLMs. The data is available at: https://huggingface.co/datasets/mustafaa/FewMMBench"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. 49 pages, 38 Figures, 5 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21854","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:07:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:07:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:07:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagi","familyName":"Shaier","name":"Shaier, Sagi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Excitation: Momentum For Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:22:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:45:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose Excitation, a novel optimization framework designed to accelerate learning in sparse architectures such as Mixture-of-Experts (MoEs). Unlike traditional optimizers that treat all parameters uniformly, Excitation dynamically modulates updates using batch-level expert utilization. It introduces a competitive update dynamic that amplifies updates to highly-utilized experts and can selectively suppress low-utilization ones, effectively sharpening routing specialization. Notably, we identify a phenomenon of \"structural confusion\" in deep MoEs, where standard optimizers fail to establish functional signal paths; Excitation acts as a specialization catalyst, \"rescuing\" these models and enabling stable training where baselines remain trapped. Excitation is optimizer-, domain-, and model-agnostic, requires minimal integration effort, and introduces neither additional per-parameter optimizer state nor learnable parameters, making it highly viable for memory-constrained settings. Across language and vision tasks, Excitation consistently improves convergence speed and final performance in MoE models, indicating that active update modulation is a key mechanism for effective conditional computation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:26Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21786","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21786","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21786","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunsuke","familyName":"Ubukata","name":"Ubukata, Shunsuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"D-COT: Disciplined Chain-of-Thought Learning for Efficient Reasoning in Small Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7, I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:08:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:44:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chain-of-Thought (CoT) distillation from Large Language Models (LLMs) often induces \"overthinking\" in Small Language Models (SLMs), leading to performance degradation and excessive token consumption. In this study, we propose Disciplined Chain-of-Thought (D-CoT), a novel framework that enforces a structured reasoning process using control tags -- such as for fact-checking and for multi-perspective exploration -- as auxiliary scaffolding during training. By optimizing the CoT trajectory, D-CoT suppresses reasoning drift and simultaneously achieves token reduction and performance improvement. We demonstrate the efficacy of our approach on Qwen3-8B: with only 5,000 training samples, D-CoT significantly boosts accuracy on GPQA-diamond by 9.9% and MMLU-Pro (0-shot) by 9.1%, while drastically reducing computational costs. Furthermore, we confirm that the model internalizes this disciplined thought structure, maintaining high performance even without explicit control tags during inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 3 figures. Code: https://github.com/gitpullpull/DisciplinedChainOfThought | Benchmarks: https://huggingface.co/datasets/gitpullpull/D-CoT-Benchmarks | Dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/gitpullpull/D-CoT-datasets"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21786","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:06:08Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21780","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21780","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21780","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zunhai","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zunhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hansen","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Hansen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyu","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Keyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Dahai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengwu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhengwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngai","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Ngai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XStreamVGGT: Extremely Memory-Efficient Streaming Vision Geometry Grounded Transformer with KV Cache Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T11:02:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:44:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning-based 3D visual geometry models have significantly advanced with the advent of large-scale transformers. Among these, StreamVGGT leverages frame-wise causal attention to deliver robust and efficient streaming 3D reconstruction. However, it suffers from unbounded growth in the Key-Value (KV) cache due to the massive influx of vision tokens from multi-image and long-video inputs, leading to increased memory consumption and inference latency as input frames accumulate. This ultimately limits its scalability for long-horizon applications. To address this gap, we propose XStreamVGGT, a tuning-free approach that seamlessly integrates pruning and quantization to systematically compress the KV cache, enabling extremely memory-efficient streaming inference. Specifically, redundant KVs generated from multi-frame inputs are initially pruned to conform to a fixed KV memory budget using an efficient token-importance identification mechanism that maintains full compatibility with high-performance attention kernels (e.g., FlashAttention). Additionally, leveraging the inherent distribution patterns of KV tensors, we apply dimension-adaptive KV quantization within the pruning pipeline to further minimize memory overhead while preserving numerical accuracy. Extensive evaluations show that XStreamVGGT achieves mostly negligible performance degradation while substantially reducing memory usage by 4.42$\\times$ and accelerating inference by 5.48$\\times$, enabling practical and scalable streaming 3D applications. The code is available at https://github.com/ywh187/XStreamVGGT/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submission to the Journal of the Society for Information Display"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21780","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:59Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:06:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:06:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21779","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21779","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21779","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyuan","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zheyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingsong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qingsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yusong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhaohong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xinqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaowei","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Jiaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Static Artifacts: A Forensic Benchmark for Video Deepfake Reasoning in Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:54:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:43:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) for deepfake detection excel at identifying spatial artifacts but overlook a critical dimension: temporal inconsistencies in video forgeries. Adapting VLMs to reason about these dynamic cues remains a distinct challenge. To bridge this gap, we propose Forensic Answer-Questioning (FAQ), a large-scale benchmark that formulates temporal deepfake analysis as a multiple-choice task. FAQ introduces a three-level hierarchy to progressively evaluate and equip VLMs with forensic capabilities: (1) Facial Perception, testing the ability to identify static visual artifacts; (2) Temporal Deepfake Grounding, requiring the localization of dynamic forgery artifacts across frames; and (3) Forensic Reasoning, challenging models to synthesize evidence for final authenticity verdicts. We evaluate a range of VLMs on FAQ and generate a corresponding instruction-tuning set, FAQ-IT. Extensive experiments show that models fine-tuned on FAQ-IT achieve advanced performance on both in-domain and cross-dataset detection benchmarks. Ablation studies further validate the impact of our key design choices, confirming that FAQ is the driving force behind the temporal reasoning capabilities of these VLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 9 figures. Submitted to CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21779","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21768","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21768","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21768","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omayra Yago","familyName":"Nieto","name":"Nieto, Omayra Yago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonardo","familyName":"Colombo","name":"Colombo, Leonardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning-Based Geometric Leader-Follower Control for Cooperative Rigid-Payload Transport with Aerial Manipulators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:39:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:42:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a learning-based tracking control framework for cooperative transport of a rigid payload by multiple aerial manipulators under rigid grasp constraints. A unified geometric model is developed, yielding a coupled agent--payload differential--algebraic system that explicitly captures contact wrenches, payload dynamics, and internal force redundancy. A leader--follower architecture is adopted in which a designated leader generates a desired payload wrench based on geometric tracking errors, while the remaining agents realize this wrench through constraint-consistent force allocation.\n Unknown disturbances and modeling uncertainties are compensated using Gaussian Process (GP) regression. High-probability bounds on the learning error are explicitly incorporated into the control design, combining GP feedforward compensation with geometric feedback. Lyapunov analysis establishes uniform ultimate boundedness of the payload tracking errors with high probability, with an ultimate bound that scales with the GP predictive uncertainty."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21768","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:42Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21763","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21763","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21763","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changxing","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Changxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Implicit Discourse Relation Recognition with Natural Language Explanations from LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:28:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:41:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implicit Discourse Relation Recognition (IDRR) remains a challenging task due to the requirement for deep semantic understanding in the absence of explicit discourse markers. A further limitation is that existing methods only predict relations without providing any supporting explanations. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have shown strong reasoning capabilities in both deep language understanding and natural language explanation generation. In this work, we propose a simple yet effective approach to distill the reasoning capabilities of LLMs into lightweight IDRR models to improve both performance and interpretability. Specifically, we first prompt an LLM to generate explanations for each training instance conditioned on its gold label. Then, we introduce a novel classification-generation framework that jointly performs relation prediction and explanation generation, and train it with the additional supervision of LLM-generated explanations. Our framework is plug-and-play, enabling easy integration with most existing IDRR models. Experimental results on PDTB demonstrate that our approach significantly improves IDRR performance, while human evaluation further confirms that the generated explanations enhance model interpretability. Furthermore, we validate the generality of our approach on sentiment classification and natural language inference"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"AAAI26'0ral"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21763","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:35Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21756","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21756","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21756","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deogyong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Deogyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junseong","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Junseong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeongeun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jeongeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhoe","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Changhoe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junguel","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Junguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jungseok","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jungseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongha","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dongha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Offline Reasoning for Efficient Recommendation: LLM-Empowered Persona-Profiled Item Indexing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T10:14:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:41:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) offer new opportunities for recommender systems by capturing the nuanced semantics of user interests and item characteristics through rich semantic understanding and contextual reasoning. In particular, LLMs have been employed as rerankers that reorder candidate items based on inferred user-item relevance. However, these approaches often require expensive online inference-time reasoning, leading to high latency that hampers real-world deployment. In this work, we introduce Persona4Rec, a recommendation framework that performs offline reasoning to construct interpretable persona representations of items, enabling lightweight and scalable real-time inference. In the offline stage, Persona4Rec leverages LLMs to reason over item reviews, inferring diverse user motivations that explain why different types of users may engage with an item; these inferred motivations are materialized as persona representations, providing multiple, human-interpretable views of each item. Unlike conventional approaches that rely on a single item representation, Persona4Rec learns to align user profiles with the most plausible item-side persona through a dedicated encoder, effectively transforming user-item relevance into user-persona relevance. At the online stage, this persona-profiled item index allows fast relevance computation without invoking expensive LLM reasoning. Extensive experiments show that Persona4Rec achieves performance comparable to recent LLM-based rerankers while substantially reducing inference time. Moreover, qualitative analysis confirms that persona representations not only drive efficient scoring but also provide intuitive, review-grounded explanations. These results demonstrate that Persona4Rec offers a practical and interpretable solution for next-generation recommender systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21756","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:05:24Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:05:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:05:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21738","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21738","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21738","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pelin","familyName":"Sekercioglu","name":"Sekercioglu, Pelin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angela","familyName":"Fontan","name":"Fontan, Angela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimos V.","familyName":"Dimarogonas","name":"Dimarogonas, Dimos V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stability of Open Multi-agent Systems over Dynamic Signed Digraphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:48:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:39:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We address the synchronization problem in open multi-agent systems (OMAS) containing both cooperative and antagonistic interactions. In these systems, agents can join or leave the network over time, and the interaction structure may evolve accordingly. To capture these dynamical structural changes, we represent the network as a switched system interconnected over a dynamic and directed signed graph. Additionally, the network may contain one or multiple leader groups that influence the behavior of the remaining agents. In general, we show that the OMAS exhibit a more general form of synchronization, including trivial consensus, bipartite consensus and containment. Our approach uses the signed edge-based agreement protocol, and constructs strict Lyapunov functions for signed networks described by signed edge-Laplacian matrices containing multiple zero eigenvalues. Numerical simulations validate our theoretical results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21738","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:57Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21735","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21735","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21735","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadrien","familyName":"Reynaud","name":"Reynaud, Hadrien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim Ethem","familyName":"Hamamci","name":"Hamamci, Ibrahim Ethem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sezgin","familyName":"Er","name":"Er, Sezgin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suprosanna","familyName":"Shit","name":"Shit, Suprosanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bjoern","familyName":"Menze","name":"Menze, Bjoern","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard","familyName":"Kainz","name":"Kainz, Bernhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SigVLP: Sigmoid Volume-Language Pre-Training for Self-Supervised CT-Volume Adaptive Representation Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:44:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:39:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale, volumetric medical imaging datasets typically aggregate scans from different vendors and devices, resulting in highly variable resolution, slice thicknesses, and numbers of slices per study. Consequently, training representation models usually requires cropping or interpolating along the z-axis to obtain fixed-size blocks, which inevitably causes information loss. We propose a new training approach to overcome this limitation. Instead of absolute position embeddings, we interpret volumes as sequences of 3D chunks and adopt Rotary Position Embeddings, allowing us to treat the z-axis as an unconstrained temporal dimensions. Building on this idea, we introduce a new vision-language model: SigVLP. In SigVLP, we implement Rotary Position Embedding as the positional encoding method, which is applied directly within the attention operation, generating input-conditioned sine and cosine weights on the fly. This design ensures consistent alignment between query and key projections and adapts to any input sizes. To allow for variable input size during training, we sample Computed Tomography volumes in chunks and pair them with localized organ-wise textual observations. Compared to using entire reports for conditioning, chunkwise alignment provides finer-grained supervision, enabling the model to establish stronger correlations between the text and volume representations, thereby improving the precision of text-to-volume alignment. Our models are trained with the Muon optimizer and evaluated on a diverse set of downstream tasks, including zero-shot abnormality and organ classification, segmentation, and retrieval tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21735","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:53Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21732","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21732","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21732","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naoto","familyName":"Tomita","name":"Tomita, Naoto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suguru","familyName":"Sato","name":"Sato, Suguru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshihiro","familyName":"Takeshita","name":"Takeshita, Toshihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aki","familyName":"Furusawa","name":"Furusawa, Aki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jarred","familyName":"Fastier-Wooller","name":"Fastier-Wooller, Jarred","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun","familyName":"Muramatsu","name":"Muramatsu, Shun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshihiro","familyName":"Itoh","name":"Itoh, Toshihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michitaka","familyName":"Yamamoto","name":"Yamamoto, Michitaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solderable Microcontroller-Integrated E-Textiles using UV-Tape-Assisted Laser Patterning Technique"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Medical Physics (physics.med-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:42:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:39:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this study, we developed a UV-tape-assisted laser patterning (UT-Laser) technique that enables the simple transfer-based formation of wiring with line widths below 200 $μ$m onto textile substrates. With the rapid advancement of wearable devices capable of acquiring various types of physiological and environmental information, research on electronic textiles (e-textiles)-in which electronic components are integrated into fabrics and clothing-has progressed considerably. However, integrating high-performance, rigid electronic components onto textiles remains challenging: the diameter of textile fibers limits the formation of fine wiring, making reliable mounting of such components difficult. To address these challenges, we devised the UT-Laser technique, in which thin foil or film materials are laser vector-cut on UV tape, and the adhesive strength is controlled through UV exposure. The unnecessary portions are selectively and collectively peeled away to form fine wiring, which is subsequently transferred onto the textile substrate. This approach enables facile fabrication of fine wiring with line widths below 200 $μ$m on textiles. Furthermore, by forming fine wiring from a flexible copper clad laminate and transferring it onto heat-resistant glass cloth, electronic components can be soldered directly, allowing the fabrication of e-textile devices capable of withstanding more than 10,000 bending cycles. The prototype e-textile device fabricated using the proposed method integrates a microcontroller, USB connector, battery holder, flash memory, inertial measurement unit, and environmental sensors, and successfully acquires data related to stair climbing, respiration, and changes in body temperature during sleep."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21732","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21728","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21728","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21728","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiqi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ruiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengxi","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Zhengxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei Qiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wei Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongfeng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixin","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Haixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yunqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Explore-on-Graph: Incentivizing Autonomous Exploration of Large Language Models on Knowledge Graphs with Path-refined Reward Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:35:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:38:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The reasoning process of Large Language Models (LLMs) is often plagued by hallucinations and missing facts in question-answering tasks. A promising solution is to ground LLMs' answers in verifiable knowledge sources, such as Knowledge Graphs (KGs). Prevailing KG-enhanced methods typically constrained LLM reasoning either by enforcing rules during generation or by imitating paths from a fixed set of demonstrations. However, they naturally confined the reasoning patterns of LLMs within the scope of prior experience or fine-tuning data, limiting their generalizability to out-of-distribution graph reasoning problems. To tackle this problem, in this paper, we propose Explore-on-Graph (EoG), a novel framework that encourages LLMs to autonomously explore a more diverse reasoning space on KGs. To incentivize exploration and discovery of novel reasoning paths, we propose to introduce reinforcement learning during training, whose reward is the correctness of the reasoning paths' final answers. To enhance the efficiency and meaningfulness of the exploration, we propose to incorporate path information as additional reward signals to refine the exploration process and reduce futile efforts. Extensive experiments on five KGQA benchmark datasets demonstrate that, to the best of our knowledge, our method achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming not only open-source but also even closed-source LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21728","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:42Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21715","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21715","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21715","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhui","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenchuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Two-Stage Active Distribution Network Voltage Control via LLM-RL Collaboration: A Hybrid Knowledge-Data-Driven Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:22:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growing integration of distributed photovoltaics (PVs) into active distribution networks (ADNs) has exacerbated operational challenges, making it imperative to coordinate diverse equipment to mitigate voltage violations and enhance power quality. Although existing data-driven approaches have demonstrated effectiveness in the voltage control problem, they often require extensive trial-and-error exploration and struggle to incorporate heterogeneous information, such as day-ahead forecasts and semantic-based grid codes. Considering the operational scenarios and requirements in real-world ADNs, in this paper, we propose a hybrid knowledge-data-driven approach that leverages dynamic collaboration between a large language model (LLM) agent and a reinforcement learning (RL) agent to achieve two-stage voltage control. In the day-ahead stage, the LLM agent receives coarse region-level forecasts and generates scheduling strategies for on-load tap changer (OLTC) and shunt capacitors (SCs) to regulate the overall voltage profile. Then in the intra-day stage, based on accurate node-level measurements, the RL agent refines terminal voltages by deriving reactive power generation strategies for PV inverters. On top of the LLM-RL collaboration framework, we further propose a self-evolution mechanism for the LLM agent and a pretrain-finetune pipeline for the RL agent, effectively enhancing and coordinating the policies for both agents. The proposed approach not only aligns more closely with practical operational characteristics but also effectively utilizes the inherent knowledge and reasoning capabilities of the LLM agent, significantly improving training efficiency and voltage control performance. Comprehensive comparisons and ablation studies demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21715","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:23Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21706","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21706","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21706","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanyi","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Guanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaozhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaozhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhu","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Zhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang Han","familyName":"Low","name":"Low, Chang Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuancan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yuancan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibing","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yibing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haofeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunjiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chunjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueming","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yueming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SurGo-R1: Benchmarking and Modeling Contextual Reasoning for Operative Zone in Surgical Video"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T09:11:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Minimally invasive surgery has dramatically improved patient operative outcomes, yet identifying safe operative zones remains challenging in critical phases, requiring surgeons to integrate visual cues, procedural phase, and anatomical context under high cognitive load. Existing AI systems offer binary safety verification or static detection, ignoring the phase-dependent nature of intraoperative reasoning. We introduce ResGo, a benchmark of laparoscopic frames annotated with Go Zone bounding boxes and clinician-authored rationales covering phase, exposure quality reasoning, next action and risk reminder. We introduce evaluation metrics that treat correct grounding under incorrect phase as failures, revealing that most vision-language models cannot handle such tasks and perform poorly. We then present SurGo-R1, a model optimized via RLHF with a multi-turn phase-then-go architecture where the model first identifies the surgical phase, then generates reasoning and Go Zone coordinates conditioned on that context. On unseen procedures, SurGo-R1 achieves 76.6% phase accuracy, 32.7 mIoU, and 54.8% hardcore accuracy, a 6.6$\\times$ improvement over the mainstream generalist VLMs. Code, model and benchmark will be available at https://github.com/jinlab-imvr/SurGo-R1"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21706","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:04:09Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:04:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:04:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renshuang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Renshuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yichong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxiang","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Xiaoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenling","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Zhenling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tinglue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tinglue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AkiraRust: Re-thinking LLM-aided Rust Repair Using a Feedback-guided Thinking Switch"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:34:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:34:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Eliminating undefined behaviors (UBs) in Rust programs requires a deep semantic understanding to enable accurate and reliable repair. While existing studies have demonstrated the potential of LLMs to support Rust code analysis and repair, most frameworks remain constrained by inflexible templates or lack grounding in executable semantics, resulting in limited contextual awareness and semantic incorrectness. Here, we present AkiraRust, an LLM-driven repair and verification framework that incorporates a finite-state machine to dynamically adapt its detection and repair flow to runtime semantic conditions. AkiraRust introduces a dual-mode reasoning strategy that coordinates fast and slow thinking across multiple agents. Each agent is mapped to an FSM state, and a waveform-driven transition controller manages state switching, rollback decisions, and semantic check pointing, enabling context-aware and runtime-adaptive repair. Experimental results show that AkiraRust achieves about 92% semantic correctness and delivers a 2.2x average speedup compared to SOTA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 11 figures, accepted to DAC"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:31Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:03:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21680","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21680","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21680","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Eckel","name":"Eckel, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henri","familyName":"Meeß","name":"Meeß, Henri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hierarchical Lead Critic based Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:33:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:34:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cooperative Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) solves complex tasks that require coordination from multiple agents, but is often limited to either local (independent learning) or global (centralized learning) perspectives. In this paper, we introduce a novel sequential training scheme and MARL architecture, which learns from multiple perspectives on different hierarchy levels. We propose the Hierarchical Lead Critic (HLC) - inspired by natural emerging distributions in team structures, where following high-level objectives combines with low-level execution. HLC demonstrates that introducing multiple hierarchies, leveraging local and global perspectives, can lead to improved performance with high sample efficiency and robust policies. Experimental results conducted on cooperative, non-communicative, and partially observable MARL benchmarks demonstrate that HLC outperforms single hierarchy baselines and scales robustly with increasing amounts of agents and difficulty."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 10 Figures, Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21680","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:29Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:03:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21677","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21677","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21677","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenxiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhenxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sirui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jieyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jieyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Chuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trie-Aware Transformers for Generative Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T08:25:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:34:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative recommendation (GR) aligns with advances in generative AI by casting next-item prediction as token-level generation rather than score-based ranking. Most GR methods adopt a two-stage pipeline: (i) \\textit{item tokenization}, which maps each item to a sequence of discrete, hierarchically organized tokens; and (ii) \\textit{autoregressive generation}, which predicts the next item's tokens conditioned on the tokens of user's interaction history. Although hierarchical tokenization induces a prefix tree (trie) over items, standard autoregressive modeling with conventional Transformers often flattens item tokens into a linear stream and overlooks the underlying topology.\n To address this, we propose TrieRec, a trie-aware generative recommendation method that augments Transformers with structural inductive biases via two positional encodings. First, a \\textit{trie-aware absolute positional encoding} aggregates a token's (node's) local structural context (\\eg depth, ancestors, and descendants) into the token representation. Second, a \\textit{topology-aware relative positional encoding} injects pairwise structural relations into self-attention to capture topology-induced semantic relatedness. TrieRec is also model-agnostic, efficient, and hyperparameter-free. In our experiments, we implement TrieRec within three representative GR backbones, achieving notably improvements of 8.83\\% on average across four real-world datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21677","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:03:24Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:03:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:03:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21653","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21653","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21653","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abas","familyName":"Bertina","name":"Bertina, Abas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Shakeri","name":"Shakeri, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Irresponsible Counselors: Large Language Models and the Loneliness of Modern Humans"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:29:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have rapidly shifted from peripheral assistive tools to constant companions in everyday and even high stakes human decision making. Many users now consult these models about health, intimate relationships, finance, education, and identity, because LLMs are, in practice, multi domain, inexpensive, always available, and seemingly nonjudgmental. At the same time, from a technical perspective these models rely on transformer architectures, exhibit highly unpredictable behavior in detail, and are fundamentally stateless; conceptually, they lack any real subjectivity, intention, or responsibility. This article argues that the combination of this technical architecture with the social position of LLMs as multis pecialist counselors in an age of human loneliness produces a new kind of advisory intimacy without a subject. In this new relation, model outputs are experienced as if they contained deep understanding, neutrality, emotional support, and user level control, while at the deeper level there is no human agent who is straightforwardly responsible or answerable. By reviewing dominant strands of AI ethics critique, we show that focusing only on developer liability, data bias, or emotional attachment to chatbots is insufficient to capture this configuration. We then explore the ethical and political implications of this advisory intimacy without a subject for policy-making, for justice in access to counseling, and for how we understand loneliness in the contemporary world."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. XX pages, X figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21653","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21652","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21652","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21652","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minhao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Minhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhikai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuewen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xuewen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengjuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Mengjuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyi","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Qingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sparsity Induction for Accurate Post-Training Pruning of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:25:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have demonstrated capabilities in text generation, while their increasing parameter scales present challenges in computational and memory efficiency. Post-training sparsity (PTS), which reduces model cost by removing weights from dense networks, is an effective approach. However, native dense matrices lack high sparsity, making existing approaches that directly remove weights disrupt model states, resulting in unsatisfactory performance recovery even with post-tuning. We propose Sparsity Induction, which promotes models toward higher sparsity at both distribution and feature levels before pruning, to push the limits of PTS. At the distribution level, we enhance distributional sparsity through mathematically equivalent scaling transformations, which are fully absorbable and incur no extra parameters or inference-time overhead. At the feature level, we introduce Spectral Norm Loss to promote feature sparsity from a low-rank perspective. Experiments across diverse model architectures and tasks demonstrate that our method further enhances sparsity-friendliness, achieving superior pruning performance over existing approaches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 1 figure, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21652","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:43Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21650","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21650","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21650","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PPCR-IM: A System for Multi-layer DAG-based Public Policy Consequence Reasoning and Social Indicator Mapping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:23:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:32:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Public policy decisions are typically justified using a narrow set of headline indicators, leaving many downstream social impacts unstructured and difficult to compare across policies. We propose PPCR-IM, a system for multi-layer DAG-based consequence reasoning and social indicator mapping that addresses this gap. Given a policy description and its context, PPCR-IM uses an LLM-driven, layer-wise generator to construct a directed acyclic graph of intermediate consequences, allowing child nodes to have multiple parents to capture joint influences. A mapping module then aligns these nodes to a fixed indicator set and assigns one of three qualitative impact directions: increase, decrease, or ambiguous change. For each policy episode, the system outputs a structured record containing the DAG, indicator mappings, and three evaluation measures: an expected-indicator coverage score, a discovery rate for overlooked but relevant indicators, and a relative focus ratio comparing the systems coverage to that of the government. PPCR-IM is available both as an online demo and as a configurable XLSX-to-JSON batch pipeline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21650","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:40Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Man","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Man","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yunyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncertainty Modeling for SysML v2"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T07:10:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:31:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Uncertainty is inherent in modern engineered systems, including cyber-physical systems, autonomous systems, and large-scale software-intensive infrastructures (such as microservice-based systems) operating in dynamic and partially observable environments. The recent publication of Precise Semantics for Uncertainty Modeling (PSUM) by the Object Management Group represents the first standardized specification for uncertainty modeling within the Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) community, providing formally defined semantics for representing and reasoning about uncertainty in models. In parallel, the second version of Systems Modeling Language (SysML v2) was released as the next-generation systems modeling language, offering improved semantic rigor and reusability, yet lacking native constructs aligned with PSUM for first-class uncertainty representation. This paper proposes a systematic extension of SysML v2 that incorporates the PSUM metamodel into its modeling framework. The extension enables explicit specification of indeterminacy sources, structured characterization of uncertainties, and consistent propagation of uncertainty within system models, while preserving conformance with SysML v2 syntax and semantics. We validate the approach through seven case studies. Results demonstrate that the proposed extension (PSUM-SysMLv2) is expressive and applicable for uncertainty-aware MBSE, and potentially enables uncertainty and uncertainty propagation analyses."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:25Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21634","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21634","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21634","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaowei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huazhu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Huazhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congde","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Congde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qirui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoqing","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Guoqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank Youhua","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Frank Youhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengzhuo","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Mengzhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentLTV: An Agent-Based Unified Search-and-Evolution Framework for Automated Lifetime Value Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.5; H.4.2; I.2.6; I.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:58:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Lifetime Value (LTV) prediction is critical in advertising, recommender systems, and e-commerce. In practice, LTV data patterns vary across decision scenarios. As a result, practitioners often build complex, scenario-specific pipelines and iterate over feature processing, objective design, and tuning. This process is expensive and hard to transfer. We propose AgentLTV, an agent-based unified search-and-evolution framework for automated LTV modeling. AgentLTV treats each candidate solution as an {executable pipeline program}. LLM-driven agents generate code, run and repair pipelines, and analyze execution feedback. Two decision agents coordinate a two-stage search. The Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) stage explores a broad space of modeling choices under a fixed budget, guided by the Polynomial Upper Confidence bounds for Trees criterion and a Pareto-aware multi-metric value function. The Evolutionary Algorithm (EA) stage refines the best MCTS program via island-based evolution with crossover, mutation, and migration. Experiments on a large-scale proprietary dataset and a public benchmark show that AgentLTV consistently discovers strong models across ranking and error metrics. Online bucket-level analysis further indicates improved ranking consistency and value calibration, especially for high-value and negative-LTV segments. We summarize practitioner-oriented takeaways: use MCTS for rapid adaptation to new data patterns, use EA for stable refinement, and validate deployment readiness with bucket-level ranking and calibration diagnostics. The proposed AgentLTV has been successfully deployed online."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 4 figures, submitted to KDD 2026: 32nd ACM SIGKDD Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining, ADS Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21634","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:02:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:02:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:02:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21622","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21622","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21622","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Enyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dandan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ADM-DP: Adaptive Dynamic Modality Diffusion Policy through Vision-Tactile-Graph Fusion for Multi-Agent Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:35:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:29:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent robotic manipulation remains challenging due to the combined demands of coordination, grasp stability, and collision avoidance in shared workspaces. To address these challenges, we propose the Adaptive Dynamic Modality Diffusion Policy (ADM-DP), a framework that integrates vision, tactile, and graph-based (multi-agent pose) modalities for coordinated control. ADM-DP introduces four key innovations. First, an enhanced visual encoder merges RGB and point-cloud features via Feature-wise Linear Modulation (FiLM) modulation to enrich perception. Second, a tactile-guided grasping strategy uses Force-Sensitive Resistor (FSR) feedback to detect insufficient contact and trigger corrective grasp refinement, improving grasp stability. Third, a graph-based collision encoder leverages shared tool center point (TCP) positions of multiple agents as structured kinematic context to maintain spatial awareness and reduce inter-agent interference. Fourth, an Adaptive Modality Attention Mechanism (AMAM) dynamically re-weights modalities according to task context, enabling flexible fusion. For scalability and modularity, a decoupled training paradigm is employed in which agents learn independent policies while sharing spatial information. This maintains low interdependence between agents while retaining collective awareness. Across seven multi-agent tasks, ADM-DP achieves 12-25% performance gains over state-of-the-art baselines. Ablation studies show the greatest improvements in tasks requiring multiple sensory modalities, validating our adaptive fusion strategy and demonstrating its robustness for diverse manipulation scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21622","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21619","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21619","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21619","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muku","familyName":"Akasaka","name":"Akasaka, Muku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soyeon Caren","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Soyeon Caren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When More Is Less: A Systematic Analysis of Spatial and Commonsense Information for Visual Spatial Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:22:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:29:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual spatial reasoning (VSR) remains challenging for modern vision-language models (VLMs), despite advances in multimodal architectures. A common strategy is to inject additional information at inference time, such as explicit spatial cues, external commonsense knowledge, or chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning instructions. However, it remains unclear when such information genuinely improves reasoning and when it introduces noise. In this paper, we conduct a hypothesis-driven analysis of information injection for VSR across three representative VLMs and two public benchmarks. We examine (i) the type and number of spatial contexts, (ii) the amount and relevance of injected commonsense knowledge, and (iii) the interaction between spatial grounding and CoT prompting. Our results reveal a consistent pattern: more information does not necessarily yield better reasoning. Targeted single spatial cues outperform multi-context aggregation, excessive or weakly relevant commonsense knowledge degrades performance, and CoT prompting improves accuracy only when spatial grounding is sufficiently precise. These findings highlight the importance of selective, task-aligned information injection and provide practical guidance for designing reliable multimodal reasoning pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 6 figures, Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21619","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:49Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21611","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21611","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21611","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangning","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Kangning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wencong","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Wencong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structurally Aligned Subtask-Level Memory for Software Engineering Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:13:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:29:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated significant potential as autonomous software engineering (SWE) agents. Recent work has further explored augmenting these agents with memory mechanisms to support long-horizon reasoning. However, these approaches typically operate at a coarse instance granularity, treating the entire problem-solving episode as the atomic unit of storage and retrieval. We empirically demonstrate that instance-level memory suffers from a fundamental granularity mismatch, resulting in misguided retrieval when tasks with similar surface descriptions require distinct reasoning logic at specific stages. To address this, we propose Structurally Aligned Subtask-Level Memory, a method that aligns memory storage, retrieval, and updating with the agent's functional decomposition. Extensive experiments on SWE-bench Verified demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms both vanilla agents and strong instance-level memory baselines across diverse backbones, improving mean Pass@1 over the vanilla agent by +4.7 pp on average (e.g., +6.8 pp on Gemini 2.5 Pro). Performance gains grow with more interaction steps, showing that leveraging past experience benefits long-horizon reasoning in complex software engineering tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21611","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:36Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21604","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21604","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21604","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiange","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qiange","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingqi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Autonomous Graph Data Analytics with Analytics-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T06:09:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:28:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper argues that reliable end-to-end graph data analytics cannot be achieved by retrieval- or code-generation-centric LLM agents alone. Although large language models (LLMs) provide strong reasoning capabilities, practical graph analytics for non-expert users requires explicit analytical grounding to support intent-to-execution translation, task-aware graph construction, and reliable execution across diverse graph algorithms. We envision Analytics-Augmented Generation (AAG) as a new paradigm that treats analytical computation as a first-class concern and positions LLMs as knowledge-grounded analytical coordinators. By integrating knowledge-driven task planning, algorithm-centric LLM-analytics interaction, and task-aware graph construction, AAG enables end-to-end graph analytics pipelines that translate natural-language user intent into automated execution and interpretable results."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21604","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:25Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21600","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21600","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21600","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ganap Ashit","familyName":"Tewary","name":"Tewary, Ganap Ashit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nrusinga Charan","familyName":"Gantayat","name":"Gantayat, Nrusinga Charan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeff","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jeff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AQR-HNSW: Accelerating Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search via Density-aware Quantization and Multi-stage Re-ranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:58:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:28:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search has become fundamental to modern AI infrastructure, powering recommendation systems, search engines, and large language models across industry leaders from Google to OpenAI. Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) graphs have emerged as the dominant ANN algorithm, widely adopted in production systems due to their superior recall versus latency balance. However, as vector databases scale to billions of embeddings, HNSW faces critical bottlenecks: memory consumption expands, distance computation overhead dominates query latency, and it suffers suboptimal performance on heterogeneous data distributions. This paper presents Adaptive Quantization and Rerank HNSW (AQR-HNSW), a novel framework that synergistically integrates three strategies to enhance HNSW scalability. AQR-HNSW introduces (1) density-aware adaptive quantization, achieving 4x compression while preserving distance relationships; (2) multi-state re-ranking that reduces unnecessary computations by 35%; and (3) quantization-optimized SIMD implementations delivering 16-64 operations per cycle across architectures. Evaluation on standard benchmarks demonstrates 2.5-3.3x higher queries per second (QPS) than state-of-the-art HNSW implementations while maintaining over 98% recall, with 75% memory reduction for the index graph and 5x faster index construction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at DAC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21600","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:18Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21598","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21598","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21598","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Touseef","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Touseef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laila","familyName":"Cure","name":"Cure, Laila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Souvika","familyName":"Sarkar","name":"Sarkar, Souvika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Retrieval Challenges in Low-Resource Public Service Information: A Case Study on Food Pantry Access"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:48:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:28:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Public service information systems are often fragmented, inconsistently formatted, and outdated. These characteristics create low-resource retrieval environments that hinder timely access to critical services. We investigate retrieval challenges in such settings through the domain of food pantry access, a socially urgent problem given persistent food insecurity. We develop an AI-powered conversational retrieval system that scrapes and indexes publicly available pantry data and employs a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline to support natural language queries via a web interface. We conduct a pilot evaluation study using community-sourced queries to examine system behavior in realistic scenarios. Our analysis reveals key limitations in retrieval robustness, handling underspecified queries, and grounding over inconsistent knowledge bases. This ongoing work exposes fundamental IR challenges in low-resource environments and motivates future research on robust conversational retrieval to improve access to critical public resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"3 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21598","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:15Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21597","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21597","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21597","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwei","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong Ting","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Hong Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NGDB-Zoo: Towards Efficient and Scalable Neural Graph Databases Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:46:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:27:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural Graph Databases (NGDBs) facilitate complex logical reasoning over incomplete knowledge structures, yet their training efficiency and expressivity are constrained by rigid query-level batching and structure-exclusive embeddings. We present NGDB-Zoo, a unified framework that resolves these bottlenecks by synergizing operator-level training with semantic augmentation. By decoupling logical operators from query topologies, NGDB-Zoo transforms the training loop into a dynamically scheduled data-flow execution, enabling multi-stream parallelism and achieving a $1.8\\times$ - $6.8\\times$ throughput compared to baselines. Furthermore, we formalize a decoupled architecture to integrate high-dimensional semantic priors from Pre-trained Text Encoders (PTEs) without triggering I/O stalls or memory overflows. Extensive evaluations on six benchmarks, including massive graphs like ogbl-wikikg2 and ATLAS-Wiki, demonstrate that NGDB-Zoo maintains high GPU utilization across diverse logical patterns and significantly mitigates representation friction in hybrid neuro-symbolic reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21597","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21593","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21593","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21593","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaojiao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jiaojiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breaking Semantic-Aware Watermarks via LLM-Guided Coherence-Preserving Semantic Injection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:38:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:27:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative images have proliferated on Web platforms in social media and online copyright distribution scenarios, and semantic watermarking has increasingly been integrated into diffusion models to support reliable provenance tracking and forgery prevention for web content. Traditional noise-layer-based watermarking, however, remains vulnerable to inversion attacks that can recover embedded signals. To mitigate this, recent content-aware semantic watermarking schemes bind watermark signals to high-level image semantics, constraining local edits that would otherwise disrupt global coherence. Yet, large language models (LLMs) possess structured reasoning capabilities that enable targeted exploration of semantic spaces, allowing locally fine-grained but globally coherent semantic alterations that invalidate such bindings. To expose this overlooked vulnerability, we introduce a Coherence-Preserving Semantic Injection (CSI) attack that leverages LLM-guided semantic manipulation under embedding-space similarity constraints. This alignment enforces visual-semantic consistency while selectively perturbing watermark-relevant semantics, ultimately inducing detector misclassification. Extensive empirical results show that CSI consistently outperforms prevailing attack baselines against content-aware semantic watermarking, revealing a fundamental security weakness of current semantic watermark designs when confronted with LLM-driven semantic perturbations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by The Web Conference 2026 (Short Paper Track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21593","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:01:07Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21588","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21588","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21588","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharv","familyName":"Murgai","name":"Murgai, Sharv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Utkarsh","familyName":"Utkarsh","name":"Utkarsh, Utkarsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle C.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Kyle C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Edelman","name":"Edelman, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erin C. S.","familyName":"Acquesta","name":"Acquesta, Erin C. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher Vincent","familyName":"Rackauckas","name":"Rackauckas, Christopher Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ABM-UDE: Developing Surrogates for Epidemic Agent-Based Models via Scientific Machine Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T05:19:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:26:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agent-based epidemic models (ABMs) encode behavioral and policy heterogeneity but are too slow for nightly hospital planning. We develop county-ready surrogates that learn directly from exascale ABM trajectories using Universal Differential Equations (UDEs): mechanistic SEIR-family ODEs with a neural-parameterized contact rate $κ_ϕ(u,t)$ (no additive residual). Our contributions are threefold: we adapt multiple shooting and an observer-based prediction-error method (PEM) to stabilize identification of neural-augmented epidemiological dynamics across intervention-driven regime shifts; we enforce positivity and mass conservation and show the learned contact-rate parameterization yields a well-posed vector field; and we quantify accuracy, calibration, and compute against ABM ensembles and UDE baselines. On a representative ExaEpi scenario, PEM-UDE reduces mean MSE by 77% relative to single-shooting UDE (3.00 vs. 13.14) and by 20% relative to MS-UDE (3.75). Reliability improves in parallel: empirical coverage of ABM $10$-$90$% and $25$-$75$% bands rises from 0.68/0.43 (UDE) and 0.79/0.55 (MS-UDE) to 0.86/0.61 with PEM-UDE and 0.94/0.69 with MS+PEM-UDE, indicating calibrated uncertainty rather than overconfident fits. Inference runs in seconds on commodity CPUs (20-35 s per $\\sim$90-day forecast), enabling nightly ''what-if'' sweeps on a laptop. Relative to a $\\sim$100 CPU-hour ABM reference run, this yields $\\sim10^{4}\\times$ lower wall-clock per scenario. This closes the realism-cadence gap, supports threshold-aware decision-making (e.g., maintaining ICU occupancy $\u0026lt;75$%), preserves mechanistic interpretability, and enables calibrated, risk-aware scenario planning on standard institutional hardware. Beyond epidemics, the ABM$\\to$UDE recipe provides a portable path to distill agent-based simulators into fast, trustworthy surrogates for other scientific domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21588","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:59Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:01:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:01:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21553","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21553","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21553","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqing","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Wenqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitri","familyName":"Kalaev","name":"Kalaev, Dmitri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah","familyName":"Fatsi","name":"Fatsi, Noah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Barcklow","name":"Barcklow, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Reinert","name":"Reinert, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Melnyk","name":"Melnyk, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Senthil","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Senthil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C. Bayan","familyName":"Bruss","name":"Bruss, C. Bayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting RAG Retrievers: An Information Theoretic Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:19:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:24:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems rely critically on the retriever module to surface relevant context for large language models. Although numerous retrievers have recently been proposed, each built on different ranking principles such as lexical matching, dense embeddings, or graph citations, there remains a lack of systematic understanding of how these mechanisms differ and overlap. Existing benchmarks primarily compare entire RAG pipelines or introduce new datasets, providing little guidance on selecting or combining retrievers themselves. Those that do compare retrievers directly use a limited set of evaluation tools which fail to capture complementary and overlapping strengths. This work presents MIGRASCOPE, a Mutual Information based RAG Retriever Analysis Scope. We revisit state-of-the-art retrievers and introduce principled metrics grounded in information and statistical estimation theory to quantify retrieval quality, redundancy, synergy, and marginal contribution. We further show that if chosen carefully, an ensemble of retrievers outperforms any single retriever. We leverage the developed tools over major RAG corpora to provide unique insights on contribution levels of the state-of-the-art retrievers. Our findings provide a fresh perspective on the structure of modern retrieval techniques and actionable guidance for designing robust and efficient RAG systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21553","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:04Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21552","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21552","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21552","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changqing","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Changqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueru","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yueru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Changhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalizing Visual Geometry Priors to Sparse Gaussian Occupancy Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T04:16:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:24:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate 3D scene understanding is essential for embodied intelligence, with occupancy prediction emerging as a key task for reasoning about both objects and free space. Existing approaches largely rely on depth priors (e.g., DepthAnything) but make only limited use of 3D cues, restricting performance and generalization. Recently, visual geometry models such as VGGT have shown strong capability in providing rich 3D priors, but similar to monocular depth foundation models, they still operate at the level of visible surfaces rather than volumetric interiors, motivating us to explore how to more effectively leverage these increasingly powerful geometry priors for 3D occupancy prediction. We present GPOcc, a framework that leverages generalizable visual geometry priors (GPs) for monocular occupancy prediction. Our method extends surface points inward along camera rays to generate volumetric samples, which are represented as Gaussian primitives for probabilistic occupancy inference. To handle streaming input, we further design a training-free incremental update strategy that fuses per-frame Gaussians into a unified global representation. Experiments on Occ-ScanNet and EmbodiedOcc-ScanNet demonstrate significant gains: GPOcc improves mIoU by +9.99 in the monocular setting and +11.79 in the streaming setting over prior state of the art. Under the same depth prior, it achieves +6.73 mIoU while running 2.65$\\times$ faster. These results highlight that GPOcc leverages geometry priors more effectively and efficiently. Code will be released at https://github.com/JuIvyy/GPOcc."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21552","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T03:00:03Z","registered":"2026-02-26T03:00:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:00:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21543","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21543","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21543","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barah","familyName":"Fazili","name":"Fazili, Barah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koustava","familyName":"Goswami","name":"Goswami, Koustava","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Multilingual Embeddings via Multi-Way Parallel Text Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:58:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:23:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multilingual pretraining typically lacks explicit alignment signals, leading to suboptimal cross-lingual alignment in the representation space. In this work, we show that training standard pretrained models for cross-lingual alignment with a multi-way parallel corpus in a diverse pool of languages can substantially improve multilingual and cross-lingual representations for NLU tasks. We construct a multi-way parallel dataset using translations of English text from an off-the-shelf NMT model for a pool of six target languages and achieve strong cross-lingual alignment through contrastive learning. This leads to substantial performance gains across both seen and unseen languages for multiple tasks from the MTEB benchmark evaluated for XLM-Roberta and multilingual BERT base models. Using a multi-way parallel corpus for contrastive training yields substantial gains on bitext mining (21.3%), semantic similarity (5.3%), and classification (28.4%) compared to English-centric (En-X) bilingually parallel data, where X is sampled from a pool of multiple target languages. Furthermore, finetuning mE5 model on a small dataset with multi-way parallelism significantly improves bitext mining compared to one without, underscoring the importance of multi-way cross-lingual supervision even for models already pretrained for high-quality sentence embeddings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21543","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:49Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21539","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21539","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21539","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaojie","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chaojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjun","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruocheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cunyuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Cunyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Bu","name":"Bu, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VasGuideNet: Vascular Topology-Guided Couinaud Liver Segmentation with Structural Contrastive Loss"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:50:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:23:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate Couinaud liver segmentation is critical for preoperative surgical planning and tumor localization.However, existing methods primarily rely on image intensity and spatial location cues, without explicitly modeling vascular topology. As a result, they often produce indistinct boundaries near vessels and show limited generalization under anatomical variability.We propose VasGuideNet, the first Couinaud segmentation framework explicitly guided by vascular topology. Specifically, skeletonized vessels, Euclidean distance transform (EDT)--derived geometry, and k-nearest neighbor (kNN) connectivity are encoded into topology features using Graph Convolutional Networks (GCNs). These features are then injected into a 3D encoder--decoder backbone via a cross-attention fusion module. To further improve inter-class separability and anatomical consistency, we introduce a Structural Contrastive Loss (SCL) with a global memory bank.On Task08_HepaticVessel and our private LASSD dataset, VasGuideNet achieves Dice scores of 83.68% and 76.65% with RVDs of 1.68 and 7.08, respectively. It consistently outperforms representative baselines including UNETR, Swin UNETR, and G-UNETR++, delivering higher Dice/mIoU and lower RVD across datasets, demonstrating its effectiveness for anatomically consistent segmentation. Code is available at https://github.com/Qacket/VasGuideNet.git."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21539","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21533","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21533","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21533","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong Hyeon","familyName":"Mok","name":"Mok, Dong Hyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seoin","familyName":"Back","name":"Back, Seoin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Fung","name":"Fung, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoxiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Guoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning-Driven Design of Single Atom Catalysts via a Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:43:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:22:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are becoming increasingly applied beyond natural language processing, demonstrating strong capabilities in complex scientific tasks that traditionally require human expertise. This progress has extended into materials discovery, where LLMs introduce a new paradigm by leveraging reasoning and in-context learning, capabilities absent from conventional machine learning approaches. Here, we present a Multi-Agent-based Electrocatalyst Search Through Reasoning and Optimization (MAESTRO) framework in which multiple LLMs with specialized roles collaboratively discover high-performance single atom catalysts for the oxygen reduction reaction. Within an autonomous design loop, agents iteratively reason, propose modifications, reflect on results and accumulate design history. Through in-context learning enabled by this iterative process, MAESTRO identified design principles not explicitly encoded in the LLMs' background knowledge and successfully discovered catalysts that break conventional scaling relations between reaction intermediates. These results highlight the potential of multi-agent LLM frameworks as a powerful strategy to generate chemical insight and discover promising catalysts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21533","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:35Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21517","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21517","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21517","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheang","familyName":"Huai","name":"Huai, Zheang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Honglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaomeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Which Tool Response Should I Trust? Tool-Expertise-Aware Chest X-ray Agent with Multimodal Agentic Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T03:09:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:20:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI agents with tool-use capabilities show promise for integrating the domain expertise of various tools. In the medical field, however, tools are usually AI models that are inherently error-prone and can produce contradictory responses. Existing research on medical agents lacks sufficient understanding of the tools' realistic reliability and thus cannot effectively resolve tool conflicts. To address this gap, this paper introduces a framework that enables an agent to interact with tools and empirically learn their practical trustworthiness across different types of multimodal queries via agentic learning. As a concrete instantiation, we focus on chest X-ray analysis and present a tool-expertise-aware chest X-ray agent (TEA-CXA). When tool outputs disagree, the agent experimentally accepts or rejects multimodal tool results, receives rewards, and learns which tool to trust for each query type. Importantly, TEA-CXA extends existing codebases for reinforcement learning with multi-turn tool-calling that focus on textual inputs, to support multimodal contexts effectively. In addition, we enhance the codebase for medical use scenarios by supporting multiple tool calls in one turn, parallel tool inference, and multi-image accommodation within a single user query. Our code framework is applicable to general medical research on multi-turn tool-calling reinforcement learning in multimodal settings. Experiments show that TEA-CXA outperforms the state-of-the-art methods and a comprehensive set of baselines. Code will be released."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21517","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:59:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:59:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:59:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21496","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21496","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21496","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umid","familyName":"Suleymanov","name":"Suleymanov, Umid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaur","familyName":"Rajabov","name":"Rajabov, Zaur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emil","familyName":"Mirzazada","name":"Mirzazada, Emil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murat","familyName":"Kantarcioglu","name":"Kantarcioglu, Murat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Refusal: Probing the Limits of Agentic Self-Correction for Semantic Sensitive Information"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T02:09:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:17:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While defenses for structured PII are mature, Large Language Models (LLMs) pose a new threat: Semantic Sensitive Information (SemSI), where models infer sensitive identity attributes, generate reputation-harmful content, or hallucinate potentially wrong information. The capacity of LLMs to self-regulate these complex, context-dependent sensitive information leaks without destroying utility remains an open scientific question. To address this, we introduce SemSIEdit, an inference-time framework where an agentic \"Editor\" iteratively critiques and rewrites sensitive spans to preserve narrative flow rather than simply refusing to answer. Our analysis reveals a Privacy-Utility Pareto Frontier, where this agentic rewriting reduces leakage by 34.6% across all three SemSI categories while incurring a marginal utility loss of 9.8%. We also uncover a Scale-Dependent Safety Divergence: large reasoning models (e.g., GPT-5) achieve safety through constructive expansion (adding nuance), whereas capacity-constrained models revert to destructive truncation (deleting text). Finally, we identify a Reasoning Paradox: while inference-time reasoning increases baseline risk by enabling the model to make deeper sensitive inferences, it simultaneously empowers the defense to execute safe rewrites."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21496","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:39Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21477","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21477","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21477","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengding","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhengding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaifeng","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zaifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prabhleen","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Prabhleen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibha","familyName":"Murthy","name":"Murthy, Vibha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongkai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Swanson","name":"Swanson, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pancake: Hierarchical Memory System for Multi-Agent LLM Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T01:09:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:14:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we identify and address the core challenges of agentic memory management in LLM serving, where large-scale storage, frequent updates, and multiple coexisting agents jointly introduce complex and high-cost approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) searching problems. We present Pancake, a multi-tier agentic memory system that unifies three key techniques: (i) multi-level index caching for single agents, (ii) coordinated index management across multiple agents, and (iii) collaborative GPU-CPU acceleration. Pancake exposes easy-to-use interface that can be integrated into memory-based agents like Mem-GPT, and is compatible with agentic frameworks such as LangChain and LlamaIndex. Experiments on realistic agent workloads show that Pancake substantially outperforms existing frameworks, achieving more than 4.29x end-to-end throughput improvement."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21477","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:58:12Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:58:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:58:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William Youngwoo","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, William Youngwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Calvin","familyName":"Yeung","name":"Yeung, Calvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hansen Jin","familyName":"Lillemark","name":"Lillemark, Hansen Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuowen","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Zhuowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiangjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohsen","familyName":"Imani","name":"Imani, Mohsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geometric Priors for Generalizable World Models via Vector Symbolic Architecture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:41:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:14:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A key challenge in artificial intelligence and neuroscience is understanding how neural systems learn representations that capture the underlying dynamics of the world. Most world models represent the transition function with unstructured neural networks, limiting interpretability, sample efficiency, and generalization to unseen states or action compositions. We address these issues with a generalizable world model grounded in Vector Symbolic Architecture (VSA) principles as geometric priors. Our approach utilizes learnable Fourier Holographic Reduced Representation (FHRR) encoders to map states and actions into a high dimensional complex vector space with learned group structure and models transitions with element-wise complex multiplication. We formalize the framework's group theoretic foundation and show how training such structured representations to be approximately invariant enables strong multi-step composition directly in latent space and generalization performances over various experiments. On a discrete grid world environment, our model achieves 87.5% zero shot accuracy to unseen state-action pairs, obtains 53.6% higher accuracy on 20-timestep horizon rollouts, and demonstrates 4x higher robustness to noise relative to an MLP baseline. These results highlight how training to have latent group structure yields generalizable, data-efficient, and interpretable world models, providing a principled pathway toward structured models for real-world planning and reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, accepted to Neurips 2025 Workshop Symmetry and Geometry in Neural Representations"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:57Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Berk","familyName":"Çakar","name":"Çakar, Berk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minseok","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Minseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James C.","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, James C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyoon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dongyoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Regular Expression Denial of Service Induced by Backreferences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Formal Languages and Automata Theory (cs.FL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-25T00:23:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:13:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents the first systematic study of denial-of-service vulnerabilities in Regular Expressions with Backreferences (REwB). We introduce the Two-Phase Memory Automaton (2PMFA), an automaton model that precisely captures REwB semantics. Using this model, we derive necessary conditions under which backreferences induce super-linear backtracking runtime, even when sink ambiguity is linear -- a regime where existing detectors report no vulnerability. Based on these conditions, we identify three vulnerability patterns, develop detection and attack-construction algorithms, and validate them in practice. Using the Snort intrusion detection ruleset, our evaluation identifies 45 previously unknown REwB vulnerabilities with quadratic or worse runtime. We further demonstrate practical exploits against Snort, including slowing rule evaluation by 0.6-1.2 seconds and bypassing alerts by triggering PCRE's matching limit."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 8 figures. Submitted to USENIX Security 2026. For the code repository of detector, see https://anonymous.4open.science/r/slmad-EABE. For the code repository of measurements, see https://anonymous.4open.science/r/atkre-7D50"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:45Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21447","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21447","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21447","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inderjeet","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Inderjeet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikas","familyName":"Pahuja","name":"Pahuja, Vikas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aishvariya Priya Rathina","familyName":"Sabapathy","name":"Sabapathy, Aishvariya Priya Rathina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chiara","familyName":"Picardi","name":"Picardi, Chiara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit","familyName":"Giloni","name":"Giloni, Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Vainshtein","name":"Vainshtein, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrés","familyName":"Murillo","name":"Murillo, Andrés","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hisashi","familyName":"Kojima","name":"Kojima, Hisashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Motoyoshi","familyName":"Sekiya","name":"Sekiya, Motoyoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Unno","name":"Unno, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junichi","familyName":"Suga","name":"Suga, Junichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adversarial Intent is a Latent Variable: Stateful Trust Inference for Securing Multimodal Agentic RAG"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:52:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current stateless defences for multimodal agentic RAG fail to detect adversarial strategies that distribute malicious semantics across retrieval, planning, and generation components. We formulate this security challenge as a Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (POMDP), where adversarial intent is a latent variable inferred from noisy multi-stage observations. We introduce MMA-RAG^T, an inference-time control framework governed by a Modular Trust Agent (MTA) that maintains an approximate belief state via structured LLM reasoning. Operating as a model-agnostic overlay, MMA-RAGT mediates a configurable set of internal checkpoints to enforce stateful defence-in-depth. Extensive evaluation on 43,774 instances demonstrates a 6.50x average reduction factor in Attack Success Rate relative to undefended baselines, with negligible utility cost. Crucially, a factorial ablation validates our theoretical bounds: while statefulness and spatial coverage are individually necessary (26.4 pp and 13.6 pp gains respectively), stateless multi-point intervention can yield zero marginal benefit under homogeneous stateless filtering when checkpoint detections are perfectly correlated."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 2 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21447","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:28Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamed","familyName":"Poursiami","name":"Poursiami, Hamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhishun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhishun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keiland","familyName":"Cooper","name":"Cooper, Keiland","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akhilesh","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Akhilesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maryam","familyName":"Parsa","name":"Parsa, Maryam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Norbert","familyName":"Fortin","name":"Fortin, Norbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Babak","familyName":"Shahbaba","name":"Shahbaba, Babak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ConformalHDC: Uncertainty-Aware Hyperdimensional Computing with Application to Neural Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:52:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hyperdimensional Computing (HDC) offers a computationally efficient paradigm for neuromorphic learning. Yet, it lacks rigorous uncertainty quantification, leading to open decision boundaries and, consequently, vulnerability to outliers, adversarial perturbations, and out-of-distribution inputs. To address these limitations, we introduce ConformalHDC, a unified framework that combines the statistical guarantees of conformal prediction with the computational efficiency of HDC. For this framework, we propose two complementary variations. First, the set-valued formulation provides finite-sample, distribution-free coverage guarantees. Using carefully designed conformity scores, it forms enclosed decision boundaries that improve robustness to non-conforming inputs. Second, the point-valued formulation leverages the same conformity scores to produce a single prediction when desired, potentially improving accuracy over traditional HDC by accounting for class interactions. We demonstrate the broad applicability of the proposed framework through evaluations on multiple real-world datasets. In particular, we apply our method to the challenging problem of decoding non-spatial stimulus information from the spiking activity of hippocampal neurons recorded as subjects performed a sequence memory task. Our results show that ConformalHDC not only accurately decodes the stimulus information represented in the neural activity data, but also provides rigorous uncertainty estimates and correctly abstains when presented with data from other behavioral states. Overall, these capabilities position the framework as a reliable, uncertainty-aware foundation for neuromorphic computing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:26Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21442","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21442","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21442","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helen","familyName":"Jenne","name":"Jenne, Helen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Vargas","name":"Vargas, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davis","familyName":"Brown","name":"Brown, Davis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gal","familyName":"Mishne","name":"Mishne, Gal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yusu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Kvinge","name":"Kvinge, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MINAR: Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T23:38:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:12:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The recent field of neural algorithmic reasoning (NAR) studies the ability of graph neural networks (GNNs) to emulate classical algorithms like Bellman-Ford, a phenomenon known as algorithmic alignment. At the same time, recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have spawned the study of mechanistic interpretability, which aims to identify granular model components like circuits that perform specific computations. In this work, we introduce Mechanistic Interpretability for Neural Algorithmic Reasoning (MINAR), an efficient circuit discovery toolbox that adapts attribution patching methods from mechanistic interpretability to the GNN setting. We show through two case studies that MINAR recovers faithful neuron-level circuits from GNNs trained on algorithmic tasks. Our study sheds new light on the process of circuit formation and pruning during training, as well as giving new insight into how GNNs trained to perform multiple tasks in parallel reuse circuit components for related tasks. Our code is available at https://github.com/pnnl/MINAR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21442","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:57:21Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:57:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:57:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21420","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21420","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21420","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanda","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hejian","familyName":"Sang","name":"Sang, Hejian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengze","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhengze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"He","name":"He, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Overconfident Errors Need Stronger Correction: Asymmetric Confidence Penalties for Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T22:46:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:11:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has become the leading paradigm for enhancing reasoning in Large Language Models (LLMs). However, standard RLVR algorithms suffer from a well-documented pathology: while they improve Pass@1 accuracy through sharpened sampling, they simultaneously narrow the model's reasoning boundary and reduce generation diversity. We identify a root cause that existing methods overlook: the uniform penalization of errors. Current approaches -- whether data-filtering methods that select prompts by difficulty, or advantage normalization schemes -- treat all incorrect rollouts within a group identically. We show that this uniformity allows overconfident errors (incorrect reasoning paths that the RL process has spuriously reinforced) to persist and monopolize probability mass, ultimately suppressing valid exploratory trajectories. To address this, we propose the Asymmetric Confidence-aware Error Penalty (ACE). ACE introduces a per-rollout confidence shift metric, c_i = log(pi_theta(y_i|x) / pi_ref(y_i|x)), to dynamically modulate negative advantages. Theoretically, we demonstrate that ACE's gradient can be decomposed into the gradient of a selective regularizer restricted to overconfident errors, plus a well-characterized residual that partially moderates the regularizer's strength. We conduct extensive experiments fine-tuning Qwen2.5-Math-7B, Qwen3-8B-Base, and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on the DAPO-Math-17K dataset using GRPO and DAPO within the VERL framework. Evaluated on MATH-500 and AIME 2025, ACE composes seamlessly with existing methods and consistently improves the full Pass@k spectrum across all three model families and benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21420","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:56:48Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:56:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:56:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Surya","familyName":"Duvvuri","name":"Duvvuri, Sai Surya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanakya","familyName":"Ekbote","name":"Ekbote, Chanakya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachit","familyName":"Bansal","name":"Bansal, Rachit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devvrit","familyName":"Khatri","name":"Khatri, Devvrit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Brandfonbrener","name":"Brandfonbrener, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inderjit","familyName":"Dhillon","name":"Dhillon, Inderjit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manzil","familyName":"Zaheer","name":"Zaheer, Manzil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interleaved Head Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T21:05:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:08:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-Head Attention (MHA) is the core computational primitive underlying modern Large Language Models (LLMs). However, MHA suffers from a fundamental linear scaling limitation: $H$ attention heads produce exactly $H$ independent attention matrices, with no communication between heads during attention computation. This becomes problematic for multi-step reasoning, where correct answers depend on aggregating evidence from multiple parts of the context and composing latent token-to-token relations over a chain of intermediate inferences. To address this, we propose Interleaved Head Attention (IHA), which enables cross-head mixing by constructing $P$ pseudo-heads per head (typically $P=H$), where each pseudo query/key/value is a learned linear combination of all $H$ original queries, keys and values respectively. Interactions between pseudo-query and pseudo-key heads induce up to $P^2$ attention patterns per head with modest parameter overhead $\\mathcal{O}(H^2P)$. We provide theory showing improved efficiency in terms of number of parameters on the synthetic Polynomial task (IHA uses $Θ(\\sqrt{k}n^2)$ parameters vs. $Θ(kn^2)$ for MHA) and on the synthetic order-sensitive CPM-3 task (IHA uses $\\lceil\\sqrt{N_{\\max}}\\rceil$ heads vs. $N_{\\max}$ for MHA). On real-world benchmarks, IHA improves Multi-Key retrieval on RULER by 10-20% (4k-16k) and, after fine-tuning for reasoning on OpenThoughts, improves GSM8K by 5.8% and MATH-500 by 2.8% (Majority Vote) over full attention."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:37Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21351","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21351","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21351","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitrii","familyName":"Pantiukhin","name":"Pantiukhin, Dmitrii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Kuznetsov","name":"Kuznetsov, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Shapkin","name":"Shapkin, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonia Anna","familyName":"Jost","name":"Jost, Antonia Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolay","familyName":"Koldunov","name":"Koldunov, Nikolay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Hierarchical Multi-Agent System for Autonomous Discovery in Geoscientific Data Archives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:37:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:06:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid accumulation of Earth science data has created a significant scalability challenge; while repositories like PANGAEA host vast collections of datasets, citation metrics indicate that a substantial portion remains underutilized, limiting data reusability. Here we present PANGAEA-GPT, a hierarchical multi-agent framework designed for autonomous data discovery and analysis. Unlike standard Large Language Model (LLM) wrappers, our architecture implements a centralized Supervisor-Worker topology with strict data-type-aware routing, sandboxed deterministic code execution, and self-correction via execution feedback, enabling agents to diagnose and resolve runtime errors. Through use-case scenarios spanning physical oceanography and ecology, we demonstrate the system's capacity to execute complex, multi-step workflows with minimal human intervention. This framework provides a methodology for querying and analyzing heterogeneous repository data through coordinated agent workflows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 6 figures, 7 tables, supplementary material included"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21351","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:55:07Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21346","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21346","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21346","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengxuan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Mengxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vivek V.","familyName":"Datla","name":"Datla, Vivek V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoop","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anoop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfy","familyName":"Samuel","name":"Samuel, Alfy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daben","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Daben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Alignment-Weighted DPO: A principled reasoning approach to improve safety alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T20:30:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:06:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in alignment techniques such as Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF), and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) have improved the safety of large language models (LLMs). However, these LLMs remain vulnerable to jailbreak attacks that disguise harmful intent through indirect or deceptive phrasing. Using causal intervention, we empirically demonstrate that this vulnerability stems from shallow alignment mechanisms that lack deep reasoning, often rejecting harmful prompts without truly understanding why they are harmful. To mitigate this vulnerability, we propose enhancing alignment through reasoning-aware post-training. We construct and release a novel Chain-of-Thought (CoT) fine-tuning dataset that includes both utility-oriented and safety-critical prompts with step-by-step rationales. Fine-tuning on this dataset encourages models to produce principled refusals grounded in reasoning, outperforming standard SFT baselines. Furthermore, inspired by failure patterns in CoT fine-tuning, we introduce Alignment-Weighted DPO, which targets the most problematic parts of an output by assigning different preference weights to the reasoning and final-answer segments. This produces finer-grained, targeted updates than vanilla DPO and improves robustness to diverse jailbreak strategies. Extensive experiments across multiple safety and utility benchmarks show that our method consistently improves alignment robustness while maintaining overall model utility."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21346","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:59Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:55:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:55:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21319","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21319","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21319","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marion","familyName":"Neumeier","name":"Neumeier, Marion","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niklas","familyName":"Roßberg","name":"Roßberg, Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Botsch","name":"Botsch, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wolfgang","familyName":"Utschick","name":"Utschick, Wolfgang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncertainty-Aware Diffusion Model for Multimodal Highway Trajectory Prediction via DDIM Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:40:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:04:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate and uncertainty-aware trajectory prediction remains a core challenge for autonomous driving, driven by complex multi-agent interactions, diverse scene contexts and the inherently stochastic nature of future motion. Diffusion-based generative models have recently shown strong potential for capturing multimodal futures, yet existing approaches such as cVMD suffer from slow sampling, limited exploitation of generative diversity and brittle scenario encodings.\n This work introduces cVMDx, an enhanced diffusion-based trajectory prediction framework that improves efficiency, robustness and multimodal predictive capability. Through DDIM sampling, cVMDx achieves up to a 100x reduction in inference time, enabling practical multi-sample generation for uncertainty estimation. A fitted Gaussian Mixture Model further provides tractable multimodal predictions from the generated trajectories. In addition, a CVQ-VAE variant is evaluated for scenario encoding. Experiments on the publicly available highD dataset show that cVMDx achieves higher accuracy and significantly improved efficiency over cVMD, enabling fully stochastic, multimodal trajectory prediction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a conference paper in IEEE Intelligent Vehicles Symposium (IV) 2026, Detroit, MI, United States"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21319","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:54:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21317","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21317","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21317","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guancheng","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Guancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diji","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Diji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shared Nature, Unique Nurture: PRISM for Pluralistic Reasoning via In-context Structure Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T19:38:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:04:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are converging towards a singular Artificial Hivemind, where shared Nature (pre-training priors) result in a profound collapse of distributional diversity, limiting the distinct perspectives necessary for creative exploration and scientific discovery. To address this, we propose to equip models with inference-time Nurture (individualized epistemic trajectories) using Epistemic Evolution paradigm, progressing through explore, internalize, and express. We instantiate this via PRISM (Pluralistic Reasoning via In-context Structure Modeling), a model-agnostic system that augments LLM with dynamic On-the-fly Epistemic Graphs. On three creativity benchmarks, PRISM achieves state-of-the-art novelty and significantly expands distributional diversity. Moreover, we evaluate the real-world utility via a challenging rare-disease diagnosis benchmark. Results demonstrate that PRISM successfully uncovers correct long-tail diagnoses that standard LLM miss, confirming that its divergence stems from meaningful exploration rather than incoherent noise. Overall, this work establishes a new paradigm for Pluralistic AI, moving beyond monolithic consensus toward a diverse ecosystem of unique cognitive individuals capable of collective, multi-perspective discovery."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21317","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:54:17Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:54:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:54:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21278","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21278","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21278","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lixian","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Lixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Upton","name":"Upton, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoqi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhuoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengman","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shengman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koustav","familyName":"Jana","name":"Jana, Koustav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peijing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"Cirimelli-Low","name":"Cirimelli-Low, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thierry","familyName":"Tambe","name":"Tambe, Thierry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Guthaus","name":"Guthaus, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H. -S. Philip","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, H. -S. Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Heterogeneous Memory Design Exploration for AI Accelerators with a Gain Cell Memory Compiler"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T18:10:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:02:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As memory increasingly dominates system cost and energy, heterogeneous on-chip memory systems that combine technologies with complementary characteristics are becoming essential. Gain Cell RAM (GCRAM) offers higher density, lower power, and tunable retention, expanding the design space beyond conventional SRAM. To this end, we create an OpenGCRAM compiler supporting both SRAM and GCRAM. It generates macro-level designs and layouts for commercial CMOS processes and characterizes area, delay, and power across user-defined configurations. The tool enables systematic identification of optimal heterogeneous memory configurations for AI tasks under specified performance metrics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21278","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:20Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21274","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21274","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21274","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johanna","familyName":"Garzón","name":"Garzón, Johanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jhonatan S. Mora","familyName":"Rodríguez","name":"Rodríguez, Jhonatan S. Mora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harold A.","familyName":"Moreno-Franco","name":"Moreno-Franco, Harold A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal extraction with an impact on diffusion-jump pricing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:13:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study an optimal extraction problem where the agent's actions in the spot market exert an additive proportional negative impact on the commodity price. The commodity price dynamics, prior to any activity by the agent, are evolved by a drifted Brownian motion with jumps. The agent's primary aim is to identify an optimal extraction strategy that maximizes their expected net profits."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21274","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:14Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21269","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21269","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21269","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Zixian","name":"Zixian, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Group Orthogonalized Policy Optimization:Group Policy Optimization as Orthogonal Projection in Hilbert Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T12:59:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Group Orthogonalized Policy Optimization (GOPO), a new alignment algorithm for large language models derived from the geometry of Hilbert function spaces. Instead of optimizing on the probability simplex and inheriting the exponential curvature of Kullback-Leibler divergence, GOPO lifts alignment into the Hilbert space L2(pi_k) of square-integrable functions with respect to the reference policy. Within this space, the simplex constraint reduces to a linear orthogonality condition = 0, defining a codimension-one subspace H0. Minimizing distance to an unconstrained target u_star yields the work-dissipation functional J(v) = - (mu / 2) ||v||^2, whose maximizer follows directly from the Hilbert projection theorem. Enforcing the boundary v \u0026gt;= -1 produces a bounded Hilbert projection that induces exact sparsity, assigning zero probability to catastrophically poor actions through a closed-form threshold. To connect this functional theory with practice, GOPO projects from infinite-dimensional L2(pi_k) to a finite empirical subspace induced by group sampling. Because group-normalized advantages sum to zero, the Lagrange multiplier enforcing probability conservation vanishes exactly, reducing the constrained projection to an unconstrained empirical loss. The resulting objective has constant Hessian curvature mu I, non-saturating linear gradients, and an intrinsic dead-zone mechanism without heuristic clipping. Experiments on mathematical reasoning benchmarks show that GOPO achieves competitive generalization while maintaining stable gradient dynamics and entropy preservation in regimes where clipping-based methods plateau."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21269","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:53:07Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:53:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:53:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21257","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21257","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21257","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen G.","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Wen G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structured Prompt Language: Declarative Context Management for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-23T17:03:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present SPL (Structured Prompt Language), a declarative SQL-inspired language that treats large language models as generative knowledge bases and their context windows as constrained resources. SPL provides explicit WITH BUDGET/LIMIT token management, an automatic query optimizer, EXPLAIN transparency analogous to SQL's EXPLAIN ANALYZE, and native integration of retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and persistent memory in a single declarative framework. SPL-flow extends SPL into resilient agentic pipelines with a three-tier provider fallback strategy (Ollama -\u0026gt; OpenRouter -\u0026gt; self-healing retry) fully transparent to the .spl script. Five extensions demonstrate the paradigm's breadth: (1) Text2SPL (multilingual NL-\u0026gt;SPL translation); (2) Mixture-of-Models (MoM) routing that dispatches each PROMPT to a domain-specialist model at runtime; (3) Logical Chunking, an intelligent strategy for documents exceeding a single context window--expressed naturally through SPL's existing CTE syntax with no new constructs, decomposing a large query into a Map-Reduce pipeline that reduces attention cost from O(N^2) to O(N^2/k) and runs identically on cloud (parallel) or local hardware (sequential); (4) SPL-flow, a declarative agentic orchestration layer with resilient three-tier provider fallback; and (5) BENCHMARK for parallel multi-model comparison with automatic winner persistence. We provide a formal EBNF grammar, two pip-installable Python packages (spl-llm, spl-flow), and comparison against Prompty, DSPy, and LMQL. SPL reduces prompt boilerplate by 65% on average, surfaces a 68x cost spread across model tiers as a pre-execution signal, and runs the identical .spl script at $0.002 on OpenRouter or at zero marginal cost on a local Ollama instance--without modification."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"44 pages, 6 figures, 14 tables, 15 code-listings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21257","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:49Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21248","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21248","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21248","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linus","familyName":"Baumgärtner","name":"Baumgärtner, Linus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adil","familyName":"Chhabra","name":"Chhabra, Adil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcelo Fonseca","familyName":"Faraj","name":"Faraj, Marcelo Fonseca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Schulz","name":"Schulz, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BuffCut: Prioritized Buffered Streaming Graph Partitioning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-18T09:26:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:01:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Streaming graph partitioners enable resource-efficient and massively scalable partitioning, but one-pass assignment heuristics are highly sensitive to stream order and often yield substantially higher edge cuts than in-memory methods. We present BuffCut, a buffered streaming partitioner that narrows this quality gap, particularly when stream ordering is adversarial, by combining prioritized buffering with batch-wise multilevel assignment. BuffCut maintains a bounded priority buffer to delay poorly informed decisions and regulate the order in which nodes are considered for assignment. It incrementally constructs high-locality batches of configurable size by iteratively inserting the highest-priority nodes from the buffer into the batch, effectively recovering locality structure from the stream. Each batch is then assigned via a multilevel partitioning algorithm. Experiments on diverse real-world and synthetic graphs show that BuffCut consistently outperforms state-of-the-art buffered streaming methods. Compared to the strongest prioritized buffering baseline, BuffCut achieves 20.8% fewer edge cuts while running 2.9 times faster and using 11.3 times less memory. Against the next-best buffered method, it reduces edge cut by 15.8% with only modest overheads of 1.8 times runtime and 1.09 times memory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21248","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:34Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21237","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21237","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21237","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Il-Sun","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Il-Sun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Premature Dimensional Collapse and Tensor-based Execution Paths for High-Dimensional Relational Operations in Cost-Based Database Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-12T04:22:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern cost-based DBMSs frequently exhibit execution instability and tail-latency amplification when high-dimensional relational operations trigger memory-regime transitions such as hash-table spilling and external materialization. We identify a structural failure mode in which intermediate representations are prematurely linearized under memory pressure, causing disproportionate I/O amplification and phase-transition-like latency behavior. To mitigate this, we propose a tensor-based execution path that delays premature linearization and preserves higher-dimensional locality through late materialization and structured intermediate layouts. Using a modified PostgreSQL-based prototype and controlled microbenchmarks, we show that under constrained memory settings (e.g., work_mem=1MB) conventional execution can spill hundreds of megabytes and exceed multi-second P99 latency, while the proposed path maintains stable execution and reduces P99 latency to sub-second levels. Our results suggest that representation timing is a first-class design variable for execution stability, complementing traditional optimization efforts focused on cardinality estimation and operator throughput."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21237","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:18Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21232","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21232","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21232","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumin","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Sumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jisun","familyName":"An","name":"An, Jisun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongman","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dongman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Urban Vibrancy Embedding and Application on Traffic Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-07T04:34:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Urban vibrancy reflects the dynamic human activity within urban spaces and is often measured using mobile data that captures floating population trends. This study proposes a novel approach to derive Urban Vibrancy embeddings from real-time floating population data to enhance traffic prediction models. Specifically, we utilize variational autoencoders (VAE) to compress this data into actionable embeddings, which are then integrated with long short-term memory (LSTM) networks to predict future embeddings. These are subsequently applied in a sequence-to-sequence framework for traffic forecasting. Our contributions are threefold: (1) We use principal component analysis (PCA) to interpret the embeddings, revealing temporal patterns such as weekday versus weekend distinctions and seasonal patterns; (2) We propose a method that combines VAE and LSTM, enabling forecasting dynamic urban knowledge embedding; and (3) Our approach improves accuracy and responsiveness in traffic prediction models, including RNN, DCRNN, GTS, and GMAN. This study demonstrates the potential of Urban Vibrancy embeddings to advance traffic prediction and offer a more nuanced analysis of urban mobility."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21232","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:11Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21231","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21231","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21231","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramchand","familyName":"Kumaresan","name":"Kumaresan, Ramchand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ACAR: Adaptive Complexity Routing for Multi-Model Ensembles with Auditable Decision Traces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-06T23:27:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present ACAR (Adaptive Complexity and Attribution Routing), a measurement framework for studying multi-model orchestration under auditable conditions. ACAR uses self-consistency variance (sigma) computed from N=3 probe samples to route tasks across single-model, two-model, and three-model execution modes. The system is implemented on top of TEAMLLM, a deterministic execution substrate with immutable artifacts and complete decision traces. We evaluate ACAR on 1,510 tasks spanning four benchmarks: MathArena, Reasoning Gym, LiveCodeBench, and SuperGPQA, using Claude Sonnet 4, GPT-4o, and Gemini 2.0 Flash, producing more than 7,550 auditable runs. Results show that sigma-based routing achieves 55.6 percent accuracy, exceeding the two-model baseline of 54.4 percent while avoiding full ensembling on 54.2 percent of tasks. The routing mechanism is model-agnostic and requires no learned components. We also document negative results. First, retrieval augmentation reduced accuracy by 3.4 percentage points, as median retrieval similarity was only 0.167, demonstrating that experience injection without semantic alignment introduces noise rather than grounding. Second, when models agree on incorrect answers (sigma equals zero), no downstream ensemble can recover; this agreement-but-wrong failure mode is intrinsic to self-consistency and bounds achievable accuracy at approximately eight percentage points below full ensembling. Third, attribution estimates based on proxy signals such as response similarity and entropy showed weak correlation with ground-truth leave-one-out values, indicating that practical attribution requires explicit counterfactual computation. This work documents which assumptions fail in practice and provides falsifiable baselines for future research on routing, retrieval, and multi-model attribution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures. Measurement framework for adaptive multi-model routing with auditable execution traces"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21231","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:09Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21230","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21230","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21230","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyue","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jiyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiahong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irwin","familyName":"King","name":"King, Irwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRACE: Trajectory-Aware Comprehensive Evaluation for Deep Research Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-05T13:28:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The evaluation of Deep Research Agents is a critical challenge, as conventional outcome-based metrics fail to capture the nuances of their complex reasoning. Current evaluation faces two primary challenges: 1) a reliance on singular metrics like Pass@1, creating a \"high-score illusion\" that ignores the quality, efficiency, and soundness of the reasoning process; and 2) the failure of static benchmarks to quantify crucial attributes like robustness and latent capability. To address these gaps, we introduce TRACE (Trajectory-Aware Comprehensive Evaluation), a framework that holistically assesses the entire problem-solving trajectory. To counter the \"high-score illusion\", we propose a Hierarchical Trajectory Utility Function that quantifies process efficiency and cognitive quality, including evidence grounding, alongside accuracy. To measure deeper attributes, TRACE introduces a Scaffolded Capability Assessment protocol, quantifying an agent's latent ability by determining the minimum guidance needed for success. Our contributions include the TRACE framework, its novel metrics, and the accompanying DeepResearch-Bench with controllable complexity. Experiments show TRACE delivers a granular ranking that uncovers critical trade-offs between agent accuracy, efficiency, and robustness entirely missed by singular metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by WWW 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21230","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:08Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21226","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21226","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21226","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ezieddin","familyName":"Elmahjub","name":"Elmahjub, Ezieddin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junaid","familyName":"Qadir","name":"Qadir, Junaid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdullah","familyName":"Mushtaq","name":"Mushtaq, Abdullah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafay","familyName":"Naeem","name":"Naeem, Rafay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Ghaznavi","name":"Ghaznavi, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Waleed","familyName":"Iqbal","name":"Iqbal, Waleed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IslamicLegalBench: Evaluating LLMs Knowledge and Reasoning of Islamic Law Across 1,200 Years of Islamic Pluralist Legal Traditions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-02T10:30:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As millions of Muslims turn to LLMs like GPT, Claude, and DeepSeek for religious guidance, a critical question arises: Can these AI systems reliably reason about Islamic law? We introduce IslamicLegalBench, the first benchmark evaluating LLMs across seven schools of Islamic jurisprudence, with 718 instances covering 13 tasks of varying complexity. Evaluation of nine state-of-the-art models reveals major limitations: the best model achieves only 68% correctness with 21% hallucination, while several models fall below 35% correctness and exceed 55% hallucination. Few-shot prompting provides minimal gains, improving only 2 of 9 models by \u0026gt;1%. Moderate-complexity tasks requiring exact knowledge show the highest errors, whereas high-complexity tasks display apparent competence through semantic reasoning. False premise detection indicates risky sycophancy, with 6 of 9 models accepting misleading assumptions at rates above 40%. These results highlight that prompt-based methods cannot compensate for missing foundational knowledge. IslamicLegalBench offers the first systematic framework to evaluate Islamic legal reasoning in AI, revealing critical gaps in tools increasingly relied on for spiritual guidance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This manuscript has been submitted for review to Artificial Intelligence \\\u0026amp; Law"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21226","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:52:02Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:52:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:52:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21224","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21224","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21224","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuetao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuetao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xuliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinzhou","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xinzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Make Every Draft Count: Hidden State based Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-02T08:25:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speculative decoding has emerged as a pivotal technique to accelerate LLM inference by employing a lightweight draft model to generate candidate tokens that are subsequently verified by the target model in parallel. However, while this paradigm successfully increases the arithmetic intensity of memory-bound inference, it causes significant compute inefficiency: the majority of draft tokens fail verification and are discarded, resulting in waste of computation. Motivated by the goal of recollecting this wasted computation, we propose a novel system that transforms discarded drafts into reusable tokens. Our key insight is to perform auto-regressive prediction at the hidden states level and postpone the integrating token information after the hidden states generation, so the draft hidden states are not contaminated by incorrect tokens, enabling hidden state reuse. To implement such a system, first we introduce a draft model architecture based on auto-regressive hidden states, which preserves richer semantics than token-based drafters to facilitate draft repurposing. Second, we design an efficient token information injection mechanism that leverages our specialized draft model to construct high-quality draft token trees and enables resampling tokens from verification failures. Third, we eliminate the overhead hidden in our design to further maximize hardware utilization. We conducted extensive evaluations against various baselines, demonstrating up to a 3.3x speedup against standard speculative decoding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21224","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:58Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21222","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21222","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21222","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riya","familyName":"Adsul","name":"Adsul, Riya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Balachandra Devarangadi","familyName":"Sunil","name":"Sunil, Balachandra Devarangadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isha","familyName":"Nalawade","name":"Nalawade, Isha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sudharshan","familyName":"Govindan","name":"Govindan, Sudharshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Task-Aware LoRA Adapter Composition via Similarity Retrieval in Vector Databases"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-01T22:20:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Parameter efficient fine tuning methods like LoRA have enabled task specific adaptation of large language models, but efficiently composing multiple specialized adapters for unseen tasks remains challenging. We present a novel framework for dynamic LoRA adapter composition that leverages similarity retrieval in vector databases to enable zero-shot generalization across diverse NLP tasks. Our approach constructs a task-aware vector database by embedding training examples from 22 datasets spanning commonsense reasoning, question answering, natural language inference, and sentiment analysis. At inference time, we retrieve the most similar training examples, compute task similarity distributions via nucleus sampling, and dynamically merge relevant LoRA adapters using retrieval weighted fusion strategies. We evaluated four merging methods Linear, Concatenation, TIES, and Magnitude Prune demonstrating that our dataset centric retrieval approach often matches or exceeds the performance of individually fine-tuned task-specific adapters. Notably, Linear merging achieves 70.95% on PIQA and 77.62% on RTE, substantially outperforming single-task baselines (46% and 52%, respectively). Our framework requires no additional retriever training, operates with frozen embeddings, and enables efficient, interpretable adapter composition. These results suggest that retrieval based dynamic merging offers a promising direction for scalable, parameter-efficient multitask learning without requiring full model retraining for each new task."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21222","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:55Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21221","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21221","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21221","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeju","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zeju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Latent Context Compilation: Distilling Long Context into Compact Portable Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-31T08:38:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient long-context LLM deployment is stalled by a dichotomy between amortized compression, which struggles with out-of-distribution generalization, and Test-Time Training, which incurs prohibitive synthetic data costs and requires modifying model weights, creating stateful parameters that complicate concurrent serving. We propose Latent Context Compilation, a framework that fundamentally shifts context processing from adaptation to compilation. By utilizing a disposable LoRA module as a compiler, we distill long contexts into compact buffer tokens -- stateless, portable memory artifacts that are plug-and-play compatible with frozen base models. Crucially, we introduce a self-aligned optimization strategy that eliminates the need for synthetic context-relevant QA pairs. By regularizing context reconstruction task with context-agnostic random queries, we force compressed tokens to reside within the model's existing instruction-following manifold. Experiments with Llama-3.1-8B demonstrate that Latent Context Compilation preserves fine-grained details and reasoning capabilities where prior methods falter, effectively decoupling memory density from model parameters even at a 16x compression ratio."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21221","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:54Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21220","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21220","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21220","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhadip","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Subhadip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Field-Theoretic Memory for AI Agents: Continuous Dynamics for Context Preservation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-31T04:33:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a memory system for AI agents that treats stored information as continuous fields governed by partial differential equations rather than discrete entries in a database. The approach draws from classical field theory: memories diffuse through semantic space, decay thermodynamically based on importance, and interact through field coupling in multi-agent scenarios. We evaluate the system on two established long-context benchmarks: LoCoMo (ACL 2024) with 300-turn conversations across 35 sessions, and LongMemEval (ICLR 2025) testing multi-session reasoning over 500+ turns. On LongMemEval, the field-theoretic approach achieves significant improvements: +116% F1 on multi-session reasoning (p\u0026lt;0.01, d= 3.06), +43.8% on temporal reasoning (p\u0026lt;0.001, d= 9.21), and +27.8% retrieval recall on knowledge updates (p\u0026lt;0.001, d= 5.00). Multi-agent experiments show near-perfect collective intelligence (\u0026gt;99.8%) through field coupling. Code is available at github.com/rotalabs/rotalabs-fieldmem."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 6 figures. Code: https://github.com/rotalabs/rotalabs-fieldmem"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21220","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:52Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21214","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21214","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21214","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohadeseh","familyName":"Sheikhqoraei","name":"Sheikhqoraei, Mohadeseh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zainabolhoda","familyName":"Heshmati","name":"Heshmati, Zainabolhoda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeinab","familyName":"Rajabi","name":"Rajabi, Zeinab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leila","familyName":"Rabiei","name":"Rabiei, Leila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Effective Multi-Domain Rumor Detection in Social Networks Using Domain-Gated Mixture-of-Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-28T16:28:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Social media platforms have become key channels for spreading and tracking rumors due to their widespread accessibility and ease of information sharing. Rumors can continuously emerge across diverse domains and topics, often with the intent to mislead society for personal or commercial gain. Therefore, developing methods that can accurately detect rumors at early stages is crucial to mitigating their negative impact. While existing approaches often specialize in single-domain detection, their performance degrades when applied to new domains due to shifts in data distribution, such as lexical patterns and propagation dynamics. To bridge this gap, this study introduces PerFact, a large-scale multi-domain rumor dataset comprising 8,034 annotated posts from the X platform, annotated into two primary categories: rumor (including true, false, and unverified rumors) and non-rumor. Annotator agreement, measured via Fleiss' Kappa ($κ= 0.74$), ensures high-quality labels.\n This research further proposes an effective multi-domain rumor detection model that employs a domain gate to dynamically aggregate multiple feature representations extracted through a Mixture-of-Experts method. Each expert combines CNN and BiLSTM networks to capture local syntactic features and long-range contextual dependencies. By leveraging both textual content and publisher information, the proposed model classifies posts into rumor and non-rumor categories with high accuracy. Evaluations demonstrate state-of-the-art performance, achieving an F1-score of 79.86\\% and an accuracy of 79.98\\% in multi-domain settings.\n Keywords: Rumor Detection, Multi-Domain, Natural Language Processing, Social Networks, Mixture-of-Experts Model"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21214","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:44Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21212","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21212","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21212","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takato","familyName":"Yasuno","name":"Yasuno, Takato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Disaster Question Answering with LoRA Efficiency and Accurate End Position"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; K.4.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-01-28T01:53:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:00:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Natural disasters such as earthquakes, torrential rainfall, floods, and volcanic eruptions occur with extremely low frequency and affect limited geographic areas. When individuals face disaster situations, they often experience confusion and lack the domain-specific knowledge and experience necessary to determine appropriate responses and actions. While disaster information is continuously updated, even when utilizing RAG search and large language models for inquiries, obtaining relevant domain knowledge about natural disasters and experiences similar to one's specific situation is not guaranteed. When hallucinations are included in disaster question answering, artificial misinformation may spread and exacerbate confusion. This work introduces a disaster-focused question answering system based on Japanese disaster situations and response experiences. Utilizing the cl-tohoku/bert-base-japanese-v3 + Bi-LSTM + Enhanced Position Heads architecture with LoRA efficiency optimization, we achieved 70.4\\% End Position accuracy with only 5.7\\% of the total parameters (6.7M/117M). Experimental results demonstrate that the combination of Japanese BERT-base optimization and Bi-LSTM contextual understanding achieves accuracy levels suitable for real disaster response scenarios, attaining a 0.885 Span F1 score. Future challenges include: establishing natural disaster Q\\\u0026amp;A benchmark datasets, fine-tuning foundation models with disaster knowledge, developing lightweight and power-efficient edge AI Disaster Q\\\u0026amp;A applications for situations with insufficient power and communication during disasters, and addressing disaster knowledge base updates and continual learning capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21212","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-26T02:51:41Z","registered":"2026-02-26T02:51:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T02:51:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":138,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2602%2A%20AND%20registered%3A%5B2026-02-26T01%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202026-02-27T01%3A00%3A00Z%5D%20AND%20(agent%20OR%20memory%20OR%20retrieval%20OR%20reasoning)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=1000"}}